### Quest Lines ###

## MIAO-GTNH

betterquesting.questline.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAQ.name=MIAO-GTNH
betterquesting.questline.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAQ.desc=The combined MIAO-GTNH storyline: 250 quests in six zones, from first-day survival aid and AE setup, through a seven-milestone GregTech spine (Steam to LuV), deep three-mod integration with GT Steam Reborn, GT Simple Wireless Network and GT-Interesting-Thing, late-game challenges, and a repeatable exchange counter.%n%nQuests live in sources/z1-z6.json; the construction contract is ZONES.md.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAQ.name=§a§lMIAO-GTNH!
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAQ.desc=Welcome to MIAO-GTNH, a fresh coat of paint over the classic GTNH grind. This quest book walks the whole road: survival help here in Z1, an early Applied Energistics network in Z2, the steam-to-LuV main spine in Z3, and deep three-mod synergy beyond.%n%nThree MIAO mods ride along: §6GTSR§r reborn steam, §bGTSWN§r wireless EU, and §dGTIT§r the cat-coin economy. Each has its own quest line; here we focus on living through week one.%n%nOld hands: this chapter keeps the beloved starter quests of the legacy MIAO line, rebuilt item by item. Claim the gift below and get moving.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAg.name=§e§lClaim Your Starter Kit
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAg.desc=GTNH does not forgive an empty stomach or a dark first night. Before you punch your first tree, grab the basics: bread to keep the hunger bar up, torches to hold the mobs back.%n%nCheck the box, collect the kit, and you are officially a MIAO pioneer. The next stops are tools, food safety, storage, and a first taste of steam.%n%nNothing here is consumed, and nothing here is owed back. The cat just likes new faces.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAw.name=§6§lHammer Time
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAw.desc=Hand in 16 logs and the workshop pays you back in iron: an ingot stack and a full vanilla tool trio. In GTNH your first real goal is never diamonds — it is a stable iron income.%n%nKeep the iron pickaxe alive: it digs everything you need before steam, including the ores for your first bronze.%n%nThe legacy MIAO line handed out this exact care package; we keep the tradition, iron prices unchanged.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAABA.name=Tinker Time
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAABA.desc=Tinkers' Construct tools grow with you: swap parts, add modifiers, never lose the tool. Set up the two founding blocks — a Crafting Station and a Tool Station.%n%nThe Crafting Station keeps its inventory open while you craft; the Tool Station is where you build and repair Tinkers tools.%n%nThe legendary Materials and You book plus a few blank patterns come with this quest. Read the book — it is genuinely good.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAABQ.name=One Swing, Nine Blocks
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAABQ.desc=The Tinkers Hammer: one swing, a 3x3 hole. For tunneling, foundations and clearing dig sites it beats any pickaxe in the game.%n%nBuild one at the Tool Station: hammer head, large plate, tough tool rod. Mine early, mine often.%n%nHand in any hammer and the workshop rounds out your rod stock so the next one is cheaper.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAABg.name=§6§lThe Grand Furnace
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAABg.desc=The Smeltery: Tinkers' multiblock furnace where ores melt into liquid metal and double while they are at it. Two units of ore per one ore mined — in GTNH that math is life or death.%n%nThis is the legacy MIAO exchange, kept alive: bring 64 stone bricks and 32 coal, and the cats deliver 48 seared bricks. Stack a controller, a drain, and a tank on top and you are in business.%n%nReminder: the smeltery must be built from seared bricks only. Regular stone will just melt your dignity.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAABw.name=Obsidian Pick Head
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAABw.desc=Obsidian tool parts: hard to make, harder to wear out. An obsidian pickaxe head on a decent handle chews through most early ores at a pace iron can only dream of.%n%nBring 64 iron and 16 obsidian; walk away with the head itself, plus lapis and redstone blocks to spend on modifiers.%n%nThis upgrade comes straight from the legacy line, where it saved many a new pioneer from gravel-pick despair.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAACA.name=§2§lAn Apple a Day
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAACA.desc=In GTNH the hunger bar is a resource bar: full hunger means full health regeneration, and an empty one means you get to meet your bed very soon. Farm wheat early, bake bread always.%n%nGolden carrots are the veteran's snack — expensive, but the best saturation in the game before cooking mods kick in. A few are on the table for you.%n%nThe food quests continue below: a proper kitchen, a feast, and a way to never think about hunger again.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAACQ.name=Home Is the Spawn Point
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAACQ.desc=A bed is not furniture, it is a respawn anchor. Die 2000 blocks from spawn without one and you will learn why veterans carry a bed in the tool belt at all times.%n%nPlace it, sleep once, and your respawn locks to your base. The compass that comes with this quest will then faithfully point home.%n%nNote: in GTNH sleeping also skips the night — see the next quest for why that matters so much.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAACg.name=Sleep Through the Night
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAACg.desc=Survival wisdom, GTNH edition: the first nights are not for heroics. Unequipped night fights are the number one killer of new pioneers, and every death drops your hard-won gear in the dirt.%n%nFence your base, light it up, and sleep. Mobs that never spawn never need killing.%n%nWhen you do have to travel at night, torches every few blocks keep the path home visible — and hostile spawns suppressed.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAACw.name=Begone, Foul Spawns!
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAACw.desc=Torches stop spawns in light; the Magnum Torch stops spawns in a whole radius. Plant one and a huge circle of land around it simply never spawns monsters — no light level math required.%n%nPerfect for the base perimeter and that one cursed ravine behind the smeltery.%n%nExchange rate unchanged since the legacy line: 16 torches for 2 Magnum Torches. The cats stock them by the crate.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAADA.name=Hunger, Begone
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAADA.desc=The Defoliage Axe, the legacy line's beloved Healing Axe: swing it and those endless food buff stacks you accumulated get wiped clean, letting your diet start fresh.%n%nDelighted Meals are the currency here: bring 8 and the axe is yours.%n%nPair it with a real food plan and the hunger bar stops being a chore. Check the kitchen quest next door.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAADQ.name=§6§lMissing Kitchenware?
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAADQ.desc=HarvestCraft turns food into a real system, and every recipe starts with the right tool: pot, skillet, saucepan, bakeware, cutting board, mortar, mixing bowl, juicer.%n%nThe legacy iron-for-kitchenware exchange stands: 32 iron ingots, one full eight-piece set, no assembly required.%n%nWith a kitchen online, bread gives way to actual meals — better hunger, better saturation, better morale.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAADg.name=§3§lHuge, Huge Capacity!
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAADg.desc=Backpacks fill. Chests sprawl. Somewhere around the third mining trip the real question stops being what to carry and becomes where to put it. The answer, as always: drawers.%n%nBring leather, string and iron as tribute; take home two full-size Storage Drawers — 32 slots each of pure one-item-per-drawer bulk storage.%n%nThe legacy line armed you with a giant backpack. This rebuild arms you with a wall that never runs out of room.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAADw.name=Chest, But Bigger
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAADw.desc=The old chest-aggregation exchange, modernized: bring 16 vanilla chests, receive 4 Iron Chests — same single-block footprint, several times the slots.%n%nNo wrenching required: iron chests keep everything a chest does, just more of it. They play nicely with hoppers, and later with your ME interfaces.%n%nUpgrade chains exist inside the Iron Chest family too — today's iron chest can be upgraded in place tomorrow.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAEA.name=Drawer Bank
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAEA.desc=A drawer bank is the classic GTNH storage room: rows of drawers for the bulk stuff — cobble, ores, ingots — with a Drawer Controller making the whole wall one big touchable inventory.%n%nLock your drawers before they fill with random junk, and note that the controller exposes the entire wall at once.%n%nThat last property matters later: one storage bus on the controller hooks the whole wall into an ME network. Z2 will remember this.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAEQ.name=Quick Bar, Quick Belt
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAEQ.desc=The Traveller's Belt is the item the legacy line never let you miss: equip it and swap between saved hotbar loadouts on the fly — mining set, combat set, building set, one click apart.%n%nLeather, string and a little iron at the tailor's counter; walk out with the belt.%n%nIt occupies a Baubles slot, so it costs you neither inventory space nor hotbar space. Pure gain.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAEg.name=No Floating Island Needed
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAEg.desc=Slime trees: sticky wood, slime saplings that grow anywhere, and slimeballs forever. No swamp, no moon, no floating island required.%n%nThe legacy exchange survives: 8 rubber saplings from IC2's rubber trees trade for a slime sapling plus a stack of assorted slime gels.%n%nSlime gels feed Tinkers modifiers and half a dozen mods later on. The tree farm pays for itself within the week.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAEw.name=Water, Water, Everywhere
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAEw.desc=Every GTNH base is founded on water: boilers drink it, farms drink it, later chemistry drinks it by the drum. The EnderIO Reservoir is the classic answer — an infinite water source in a single block.%n%nEight buckets at intake, two reservoirs out. Place one by the farm and one by the boiler room, and the bucket brigade is disbanded forever.%n%nThe legacy line called this quest Endless Water. It undersold: water is the least endless thing in GTNH until you build this.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAFA.name=Rushing Streams
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAFA.desc=Fluids need to move: from reservoir to boiler, from smeltery drain to casting table. EnderIO liquid conduits are the beginner-friendly backbone — cheap, compact, and upgradable later without tearing walls out.%n%nGlass and iron at the counter; sixteen conduits in your pocket.%n%nRule of thumb: always lay one more conduit than the plan says. The plan always grows.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAFQ.name=Let There Be Light
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAFQ.desc=Light is base defense. Glowstone blocks are the tidy way: bright, compact, and no flickering torches on every wall.%n%nHand in glowstone dust and receive pressed glowstone blocks plus a torch bundle for the roads between builds.%n%nA bright base is a safe base — and it photographs better for the base showcase thread.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAFg.name=Gravity Does the Work
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAFg.desc=Before AE, before pipes, there was the hopper: one block, no power, moves items down and sideways forever. A chest under the smeltery drain, a hopper above it — congratulations, that is an automated casting station.%n%nFour hoppers to qualify; a chest-and-iron care package to build with.%n%nHoppers are also the seed of a habit: every manual chore you automate now is one you never do again. AE in Z2 is this idea at industrial scale.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAFw.name=§c§lYour First Boiler
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAFw.desc=Steam is where GTNH truly begins: a coal boiler feeding simple steam machines will out-produce your entire muscle-powered workshop. Place one, feed it coal and water, and listen to the future hiss.%n%nSafety lecture, once: a boiler burning fuel without water goes from kettle to crater. Keep the water supply redundant — that is what the reservoir quest two doors down was for.%n%nThe steam milestone proper lives in Z3; this is your boarding ticket. Coal stipend included.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAGA.name=Steam-Cooked Dinner
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAGA.desc=A steam furnace runs on the coal your boiler already burns — no fuel items consumed at the cooker itself, and it never burns a steak to charcoal.%n%nOne steam machine qualifies you; iron for the next one rides along.%n%nThis is the whole GTNH early loop in one block: burn cheap fuel centrally, spend it everywhere. Z3 will industrialize the idea; you are pre-qualified.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAGQ.name=Coins for the Cat
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAGQ.desc=Meet the local currency: Neko Coins. The GTIT mod runs a whole economy on them — daily sign-in bonuses, a lottery wheel, a shop, even interest for leaving coins in the bank.%n%nSign in once with the GTIT daily check-in and the cats drop two coins on the spot. The gtit quest line explains every counter and rate.%n%nCoins buy QoL rings later — there is one ring that quite literally pays your food bill. Save up.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAGg.name=§e§lA Feast of Feasts
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAGg.desc=The legendary marshmallow exchange: eight of Dezil's marshmallows — yes, those marshmallows — trade for a banquet table of HarvestCraft's finest: supreme pizza, hearty breakfast, deluxe cheeseburger, beef wellington, epic bacon, meat feast pizza, rainbow curry, delighted meals.%n%nEvery plate is top-shelf hunger and saturation. Stock a chest of these and food anxiety ends for good.%n%nOne Ultimate Stew rides on top, because the legacy line would not have it any other way.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAGw.name=Death Insurance, Cheap
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAGw.desc=GTNH deaths are mostly logistics failures: the gear you dropped is 40 blocks down a lava-flavored ravine. Cheap insurance: a backup chest at spawn holding a spare pickaxe, sword, food and torches.%n%nStage four chests at your bed and the insurer issues a spare iron kit on the spot.%n%nRespawn, re-kit, revenge. The loop of champions.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAHA.name=Fence It or Regret It
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAHA.desc=A fence is a mob filter: passive animals stay in, pathfinding problems stay out, and your wheat stops feeding every passing cow.%n%nThirty-two planks traded for a fence kit plus torches for the perimeter. Ring the base, gate the road, sleep easy.%n%nLater, walls become automation boundaries and pollution containment — good habit to build now.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAHQ.name=The Cat's Tip Jar
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAHQ.desc=Pure dessert quest: acknowledge the cat economy, receive a coin and a whole cake. No conditions, no fine print.%n%nWhile you are here — the gtit line's daily sign-in stacks with these handouts, and shimmering coins wait down that road for the ring connoisseurs.%n%nThe cake is not a lie. It is, however, mostly for the screenshot.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAHg.name=Wrench It, Don't Whack It
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAHg.desc=New pioneer, old rule: GregTech machines are picked up with a wrench, never by punching. Breaking a GT machine by hand gives you back a pile of machine husks and regret — and losing a boiler that way is a rite of passage nobody needs.%n%nCarry the GT wrench; right-click machines to disassemble them properly, covers and all.%n%nOne wrench qualifies you here. Keep it on the belt next to the traveller's belt — it never stops being useful.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAHw.name=Charcoal Economy
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAHw.desc=Coal is life — boilers eat it, furnaces eat it, and the good coal is needed for coke and steel later. The oldest trick in the book: a tree farm feeding charcoal kilns produces fuel forever, on the surface, with zero mining.%n%nEight logs in, sixteen coal out to bootstrap your first fuel stockpile.%n%nVeteran tip: never feed your last coal to a machine you cannot restart.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAIA.name=Dig Safe, Stay Alive
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAIA.desc=Mining rules that keep pioneers alive: never dig straight down, never dig straight up, and treat every gravel hiss like a loaded question. Lava lives at diamond depth, and it prefers ambush.%n%nSixty-four cobblestone proves your tunnel-lining habit; back comes a ladder-and-torch safety kit.%n%nBranch-mine at Y 40-50 for gems and diamonds, wall off what floods, and the deep dark stays profitable instead of terminal.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAIQ.name=Iron Is Currency
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAIQ.desc=In GTNH's first weeks, iron is the reserve currency: tools, machines, rails, chests, conduits — everything invoices in iron. A steady iron flow is worth more than any single diamond.%n%nTwenty-four iron ore at the counter; sixteen ingots back, smelting fee waived, coal included.%n%nTip: bank raw ore until you have a steam macerator — doubled ingots beat fast ingots every time.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAIg.name=War Rations
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAIg.desc=Long expeditions fail on food, not combat. The ration kit rule: before any trip longer than a day-cycle, pack top-shelf meals — hearty breakfasts and golden carrots, not bread rolls.%n%nBank sixteen bread and receive a proper expedition ration pack in return.%n%nHigh-saturation food means fewer stops, which means more daylight at the dig site — the compound interest of exploring.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAIw.name=§a§lSteady on Your Feet
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAIw.desc=Graduation check for the survival chapter — show, don't tell: an obsidian pick head from the tool chain, a live drawer bank for storage, and a burning boiler for steam. Whenever you are ready; nothing is consumed.%n%nIf you can tick all three, week one of GTNH no longer scares you. Ahead: Z2 hands you an entire AE network, and Z3 industrializes your steam.%n%nThe legacy line ended this chapter with a gift; so do we. Go get automated.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAJA.name=§5§lThe Gate of AE
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAJA.desc=Applied Energistics, the endgame of all storage — and, in this pack, a day-one luxury. Most packs gate ME networks behind HV circuits; the legacy MIAO line had a different idea: drop a working network on the pioneer's bench early and let automation carry the grind.%n%nThis zone honors that idea: the very next milestones hand you a starter network, then teach channels, buses, autocrafting, P2P, wireless, quantum links and fluid AE until your little network runs itself.%n%nBring curiosity. The materials asked are deliberately trivial — the cats are buying.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAJQ.name=First Contact: Fluix
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAJQ.desc=Every ME network runs on fluix: drop redstone, nether quartz and charged certus quartz into water together and they react into fluix crystals, the raw soul of AE2.%n%nYou do not need to react any yet — just show the cats you can source the mundane halves: 32 redstone and 32 nether quartz.%n%nCertus grows from crystal seeds in water, faster near a powered growth accelerator. That knowledge pays off at the end of this row.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAJg.name=§d§lAE Falls From the Sky!
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAJg.desc=The signature moment of the legacy line, rebuilt: trade four chests and sixteen iron ingots, and the sky delivers a working starter network — two ME Controllers, two Energy Acceptors, an ME Drive, two 4k storage cells, a terminal, a crafting terminal, a roll of smart cable, a Network Tool, and a pair of solar panels to feed it all.%n%nNothing here requires a single certus crystal from you. That is the point: automation first, gratitude later.%n%nKeep everything. The next quests are a guided tour of exactly these parts.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAJw.name=Powering the Baby Network
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAJw.desc=Assembly order, learned once and used forever: power into the Energy Acceptor (it converts EU, RF and friends into AE), ME Drive beside it, a smart cable on top, terminal on the cable, cells into the drive. Green lights across the front — you have a network.%n%nThe Network Tool right-clicks any part to show power draw and the parts list. Meet your new best friend.%n%nTrick: the solar panels from the gift run this whole starter network for free. No GT voltage tier required yet.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAKA.name=Channels 101
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAKA.desc=Channels are AE's bandwidth. Every device on the network — terminal, drive, bus, interface — eats one channel. Without an ME Controller the whole network is capped at 8 channels; with controllers formed, each face of the controller block tree feeds 32.%n%nSmart cables show their live channel count as colored pips, which is why the gift gave you smart and not glass.%n%nBring four smart cables to prove you are wiring, and take dense ones for the backbone ahead.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAKQ.name=Dense Backbone
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAKQ.desc=Dense cables carry 32 channels and display usage — the backbone every serious base runs on. Buses and flat interfaces cannot mount directly on dense cable; branch off with regular cable for the last meter.%n%nCable anchors stop cables from connecting where you do not want them to; quartz fiber carries power but no channels, perfect for feeding a subnet from the main grid.%n%nFour dense cables in, a keg of anchors and fiber out. Wire tidy, sleep well.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAKg.name=The Terminal Family
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAKg.desc=The ME Terminal is your window into networked storage. The Crafting Terminal adds a crafting grid with the network at your elbow. The Interface Terminal lists every interface's pattern slots across the base, sorted by machine.%n%nHold a fluid container and ctrl-click in a terminal to move fluids, too.%n%nShow the cats a crafting terminal and they round out the family with the interface terminal, plus a view cell to filter what your terminal displays.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAKw.name=It's Storage Time
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAKw.desc=Storage cells do the actual hoarding: 1k cells hold 8,192 items but choke on item types — 63 distinct types max. The bigger the component, the more types and bytes.%n%nThe ME Drive holds 10 cells and color-codes their status: green means room, orange means full on types, red means full, black means no channel or no power.%n%nTwo 1k cells earn you two 4k and a 16k — the cat subsidy on capacity.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAALA.name=Cell Workbench Day
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAALA.desc=The Cell Workbench formats cells: lock a cell to named item types and it refuses everything else — the tidy way to run one cell per material.%n%nFormatting works on fluid cells too. A formatted 4k full of nothing but iron ingots is a beautiful thing.%n%nThe workbench does not join the network, so it costs no channel. Bring one; leave with capacity cards — they widen the filter slots of buses and export interfaces.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAALQ.name=§9§lDissolve and Converge
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAALQ.desc=The two workhorse parts, straight from the legacy line: the ME Import Bus pulls items from the inventory it faces into the network; the ME Export Bus pushes configured items out to machines. Everything automated you will ever build is these two plus opinions.%n%nShow one of each and the cats fund the fleet: eight more of each.%n%nPro tip from the old line: when machines are fast, let them output into an interface instead — instant, and no bus lag.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAALg.name=Drawers Join the Cloud
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAALg.desc=Z1 built you a drawer wall; now bolt it onto the network. Place a Drawer Controller against the wall, then a Storage Bus on the controller — the entire wall becomes network storage through one channel, one click.%n%nSet that storage bus to a higher priority than your cells (higher number wins) and mining drops pile into the drawers while crafted goods land in cells.%n%nOne warning the AE line insists on: hammering a drawer controller with massive I/O hurts server TPS. Bulk in, bulk out — not a million items per second.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAALw.name=Import Bay Overhaul
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAALw.desc=The legacy line's grand import refit, rescaled for early game: field eight import buses and the cats ship sixteen — enough to give every quarry output, smeltery drain and kitchen chest its own intake.%n%nAE2FC's fluid import bus comes along free: same idea, but it drinks liquids.%n%nConfigure each bus to its payload; blank-faced import buses move anything, which sounds nice until the cobblestone avalanche.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAMA.name=Output Bay Overhaul
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAMA.desc=Mirror refit: eight export buses deployed, sixteen shipped. Fuel to boiler hoppers, coal to furnace rows, food to the kitchen — an export bus per mouth to feed.%n%nFluid export buses ride along, for pushing water to reservoir-less machines and steam to wherever it earns.%n%nAn export bus with a capacity card gains a priority icon per slot — subtle, then indispensable.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAMQ.name=§9§lInterfaces Everywhere
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAMQ.desc=The ME Interface is the universal adapter between the network and every machine ever made: it stocks configured items, accepts pattern work, and lets machines eject straight into the network. Block form or flat part form, one channel each (the block form serves several neighbors at once).%n%nThe legacy line called this quest Connecting Reality. Fair: once interfaces cover your machine hall, the network is the base.%n%nSixteen hoppers and iron at the counter, sixteen interfaces out. Retrofit everything.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAMg.name=The Molecular Assembler
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAMg.desc=Molecular Assemblers are the hands of autocrafting: feed them a pattern through an adjacent interface and they craft it, tirelessly, one recipe after another.%n%nOne assembler per interface runs a single recipe — fine to start. More assemblers with more interfaces means parallel crafts.%n%nBring one, take four plus a stack of blank patterns. The autocrafting pair of quests is next, and they are the entire point of this mod.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAMw.name=§9§lCrafting CPU
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAMw.desc=Autocrafting jobs live in a Crafting CPU: a square multiblock of Crafting Units. Storage units (1k to 64k) provide the bytes a job occupies; co-processing units add parallel assemblers to a job. One CPU multiblock runs one job, on one channel, however big it is.%n%nShape it from a frame and two 1k crafting storages; the cats add accelerator units so your first jobs run wide.%n%nRule of thumb: many small CPUs beat one giant one — jobs queue badly on big blocks.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAANA.name=Patterns and Pattern Terminal
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAANA.desc=A pattern is a recipe the network can execute: encode it in the Pattern Terminal — crafting type for 3x3 recipes, processing type with explicit inputs and outputs for machines — then park it in an interface or assembler.%n%nBlank patterns are the paper of this bureaucracy. Sixteen in the slot, thirty-two back: the cats subsidize paperwork.%n%nEncode one crafting pattern now — a chest, a torch, anything. The next quest will place your first order.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAANQ.name=Your First Autocraft
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAANQ.desc=The moment the whole zone has been building to: open the crafting terminal, shift-click a crafted item, watch the plan unfold — ingredients reserved, patterns fired, assemblers humming. Your first network-made item.%n%nIf the order fails, it names the missing ingredient in chat. The network does not gaslight you.%n%nThis checkbox is ceremony only. The cats pay two coins for attendance.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAANg.name=Crafting Monitor
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAANg.desc=From the legacy line's beloved I Will Form the Items: the Crafting Monitor joins a CPU multiblock and renders the running job — every pending step, every missing item, progress bars instead of vibes.%n%nInstall one and the cats round out the CPU with the heavy hardware: a 16x co-processing unit and a 64k crafting storage, so big orders stop queuing behind small ones.%n%nYour CPU now books jobs like a restaurant with a visible kitchen. Confidence included.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAANw.name=Pattern Supplier Kit
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAANw.desc=The legacy line's Pattern Supplier payout, in modern parts. The Pattern Optimization Matrix: place one anywhere on the network and the Optimize Patterns button appears on big orders — it splits repeated steps into parallel batches. One matrix per network is enough.%n%nWith it goes the Super Stock Replenisher: point it at stocked interfaces and it keeps their configured levels topped up — a supply chain in one block.%n%nEight blank patterns at the counter. The cats handle logistics; that is rather the theme here.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAOA.name=§b§lCompress the Channels
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAOA.desc=P2P tunnels are channel teleporters: one tunnel packs up to 32 channels into a single channel and delivers them at its twin. Each tunnel itself needs one free channel; tunnels cannot nest; a regular cable carries 4 tunnel links, a dense one 16.%n%nThe standard trick: dedicate a second controller as a transport net, feed everything into P2P at one end, fan out at the other. Base-wide channel problems end here.%n%nThe legacy line paid this quest out in memory cards, and so do we: an advanced memory card from BetterP2P links and re-links tunnels in bulk, plus a stock of tunnels and the interface-parallelizer parts the old-timers rave about.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAOQ.name=Fluid P2P
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAOQ.desc=AE2FC extends the P2P trick to tanks: fluid P2P interfaces move liquids between networks and machines without a single pipe run — water to the boiler row, steam to the machine hall, all through one channel-sized wormhole.%n%nDeploy two, receive eight. The memory card from the previous quest attunes fluid tunnels exactly like item ones.%n%nYour fluid logistics now have a budget of zero pipes. Spend it wisely, or not at all.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAOg.name=§e§lFaster Than Light
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAOg.desc=The legacy payout, at legacy generosity: eight diamonds trade for sixteen acceleration cards plus eight capacity cards. Acceleration cards speed up buses, interfaces and inscribers; capacity cards widen their slots.%n%nStacking cards stacks speed, to the point of absurdity — an import bus with a fistful of cards inhales chests per tick.%n%nYes, the old line made this trade in diamonds too. Some prices are heritage-protected.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAOw.name=Hyper Acceleration
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAOw.desc=Hyper-acceleration cards: the next tier of hurry. They only fit the ME IO Port and the fluid buses — where they hit absurd throughputs: 4,096 items per tick per card in an IO Port, 4,096,000 mb per tick in a fluid bus.%n%nOne card to qualify; two back, plus a bonus bundle of plain acceleration cards.%n%nThe IO Port they live in is two quests down. The universe will not feel the same.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAPA.name=The ME IO Port
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAPA.desc=The ME IO Port bulk-moves cells: drop cells in, set the transfer direction, and it empties or fills them against the network at machine speed — the civilized way to migrate your 1k hoard into the big cells.%n%nThe legacy line's grand bay refit compressed to one block. Slot in hyper cards and it inhales whole drives per tick.%n%nBring the port; the cats contribute two 16k cells to migrate into.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAPQ.name=Crystal Growth Chamber
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAPQ.desc=Certus independence: the Crystal Growth Chamber turns seeds and dust into pure crystals on the network's tab, no water pool choreography required. Pair it with a growth accelerator — seeds in adjacent water shimmer faster near a powered one — and the quartz supply never sleeps.%n%nPure crystals feed every processor press in AE2. This block is where late-game autocrafting is born.%n%nRedstone stipend included, because fluix never forgives a shortage.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAPg.name=§d§lA Network, World-Wide
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAPg.desc=Cut the last cable to your terminal. The Wireless Access Point is mandatory for wireless — at least one per network — and its range scales with the wireless boosters installed in it; more boosters, more meters, more power draw.%n%nDeploy a WAP and eight boosters; receive thirty-two boosters and a Wireless Terminal. Your inventory now contains your entire base.%n%nThe legacy line shipped this exact care package. It remains the single largest quality-of-life jump in the pack.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAPw.name=Wireless Crafting
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAPw.desc=The Wireless Crafting Terminal: crafting grid plus network access, from anywhere in WAP range. Stand in a field, craft a factory. The legacy line called the network complete only after this.%n%nThe magnet card vacuums drops to you automatically; the infinity booster card removes the range anxiety entirely.%n%nBring the terminal; the upgrade cards come with the house.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAQA.name=Maximum Security
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAQA.desc=The ME Security Station decides who may do what: insert blank biometric cards to register players, grant or revoke building, security and craft permissions per head. One station secures the whole network at the software layer.%n%nIt cannot stop a pickaxe — physical security is still fences and, eventually, quantum-hardened walls. But it stops strangers from emptying your cells politely.%n%nA priority card comes with this one: click buses in sequence and it increments their priority automatically. Large arrays, one minute of setup.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAQQ.name=§d§lLink Everything
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAQQ.desc=Quantum bridges connect ME networks across any distance and any dimension: build a Quantum Ring around a Quantum Link Chamber at each end, drop a pair-bonded Quantum Entangled Singularity into each chamber, cable into the ring — done.%n%nEntangled singularities are crafted in pairs and only work as that pair. Each ring draws roughly 1000 EU/t, and the far side needs a jump-start from an energy cell before it self-feeds.%n%nThe legacy line paid out rings by the crate; sixteen rings and two chambers are yours, plus two pre-bonded singularities. Your mining outpost and your factory floor now share one chest.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAQg.name=Fluid Interfaces
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAQg.desc=AE2FC's fluid interface is the ME Interface's wet sibling: patterns with fluid inputs and outputs, molecular assemblers filling and emptying containers, GT machines fed by the drum — all through the network.%n%nTwo interfaces fielded, eight shipped. Fluid autocrafting quietly becomes the backbone of chemistry.%n%nThe old line knew this well: its fluid recipes were the first thing it automated, back when item autocrafting was still a novelty.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAQw.name=Fluid Storage Cells
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAQw.desc=Fluid cells drink liquids into the network: tanks by the thousand buckets, searchable in the terminal, no puddle management. Each tier up quadruples capacity — they outscale physical tanks fast.%n%nBring two 4k fluid cells, take two 16k. The upgrade math only ever goes one direction.%n%nHold a fluid container and ctrl-click in the terminal to move fluids by hand when you must.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAARA.name=Fluid Buses
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAARA.desc=The fluid bus family completes wet logistics: fluid import buses drain tanks and machines into fluid cells; fluid export buses push configured liquids back out; the fluid storage bus mounts an external tank as network fluid storage.%n%nDeploy one of each import and export; the cats ship the fleet plus a pack of 1k fluid cells and two fluid storage buses.%n%nBoiler feedwater, smeltery coolant, chemistry feeds — one network, zero pipes to babysit.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAARQ.name=Dense Energy Boys
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAARQ.desc=Networks eat power per device — the Network Tool itemizes the bill — and big crafting orders spike it hard. Energy cells are the buffer: charge in quiet hours, discharge during order storms.%n%nThe dense cell holds several times the plain one. Bring a regular energy cell, take two dense ones.%n%nBudget power like channels: headroom today is uptime tomorrow. The quantum ring quest next door bills about 1000 EU/t per ring, prepaid by these.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAARg.name=The Level Maintainer
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAARg.desc=The Level Maintainer is set-and-forget logistics: list the items and levels you want held — 512 iron, 64 bread, 32 cases of each circuit — and it files autocraft orders whenever stock dips. The network restocks itself.%n%nIt pairs perfectly with the Pattern Optimization Matrix: big restock batches get split into parallel steps automatically.%n%nOne maintainer on the network and your supply chain stops needing you. That was the plan all along.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAARw.name=§c§lEndless AE Energy
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAARw.desc=The legacy line's finale indulgence: a Creative Energy Cell, whose internal reservoir does not deplete, plus an energy card to upgrade wireless gear to the same courtesy.%n%nThe asking price stays ceremonial — pearls, diamonds and emeralds by the bowl — because in this pack, power freedom is a milestone, not a grind.%n%nPlace the cell anywhere on the network. Then go back and delete every solar panel you ever hung out of anxiety.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAASA.name=Spatial Storage 101
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAASA.desc=Spatial storage swallows rooms whole: a frame of Spatial Pylons boxes in a region of the world; the Spatial IO Port swaps that region into a spatial cell — blocks, machines, contents, all of it — and can swap it back or into another cell elsewhere.%n%nThe 2-cubed cell in this quest holds a 2x2x2 region; bigger cells hold exponentially more. People build cargo teleporters and pocket bases with these.%n%nBring the starter cell; leave with pylons and a port. What you imprison in a cell is your business.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAASQ.name=Condense and Void
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAASQ.desc=The Matter Condenser is entropy with a GUI: feed it junk and it condenses matter toward singularity food; give it a void cell and configured trash simply stops existing — cobble beyond the 10k mark, dirt beyond decorum.%n%nVoid cells sit in drives like any cell and annihilate only what their filter names.%n%nBring the condenser; leave with two void cells and a pearl stipend. Landfill is now a config file.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAASg.name=The Cell Ladder
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAASg.desc=The cell ladder climbs forever: 1k to 64k, then the advanced series — 256k, 1024k, 4096k, 16384k — each rung quadrupling storage and type limits. Somewhere up there, item counts stop being a number you track.%n%nHand in one 64k cell and the cats promote you to the 256k advanced series.%n%nThe 1024k and beyond are waiting in the AE line proper, for when your base starts ordering in bulk.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAASw.name=§6§lGraduation: A Live Network
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAASw.desc=The Z2 viva: prove the network lives. Show the organs of a working autocrafting system — controller, drive, interface, assembler, crafting storage, both buses, and a fistful of patterns. Nothing is consumed; this is inspection, not tax.%n%nPass, and the graduation grant lands: 64k cells for the hoard, advanced crafting storage for the CPUs, a fat coin purse, and the pick of a lootbag.%n%nFrom here the AE line proper takes over — advanced cells, spatial IO at scale — while Z3 waits to industrialize your steam. You came for a storage chest; you leave with a factory that runs itself.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAATA.name=§6§lSteam Age, Ignition!
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAATA.desc=Welcome to the spine of MIAO-GTNH: seven age milestones from steam all the way to LuV. The steam age is about your first machines, squeezing more from every ore, and trading bronze muscle for steel discipline.%n%nTwo rules from the tutorial still save bases here: boilers only drink water fed from the §6bottom§r, and §cnever add water to a dry, hot boiler — it explodes§r. Steam exits from the sides.%n%nOur recommended route bolts GT Steam Reborn onto the backbone: hub array early, cache nodes everywhere, multiblocks doing the heavy lifting.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAATQ.name=First Bronze Spin
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAATQ.desc=Every empire starts with a boiler. Craft your first bronze coal boiler — the same machine the tutorial line asks for at the gates of Tier 0.5.%n%nKeep it fed with coal and water from below, and let the steam push your first machines through the copper-tin bronze age.%n%nBronze is a stopover, not a destination. Steel is waiting two quests down the road.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAATg.name=Water Discipline
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAATg.desc=Upgrade to the steel coal boiler: hotter, hungrier, and even less forgiving about water discipline.%n%nThe tutorial's warning deserves repeating in bold: §cadding water to a dry, hot boiler causes an explosion§r. Pipe water in from the bottom only, keep a buffer tank above the pump, and never let the firebox run dry while lit.%n%nA base that burns down before LV is a base that deserved it.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAATw.name=Steam Kitchen
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAATw.desc=The steam furnace cooks with pressure instead of coal flames — the tutorial's 'Using Steam to Cook Things', MIAO edition.%n%nHook it to the boiler network, feed it ore and dinner alike, and bank the coal you save for the steel push.%n%nOne furnace is a kitchen. Four furnaces on a GTSR backbone is a foundry.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAUA.name=Macerator v1.0
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAUA.desc=The bronze steam macerator doubles what you get from every ore block. This is where ore processing stops being optional.%n%nCrushed ores cascade into dusts, dusts into ingots, and your coal seam suddenly lasts three times longer.%n%nOptional side order: dump a stack of coal in the slot so the checker sees you are serious about stockpiling.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAUQ.name=Boards Before Circuits
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAUQ.desc=Electronics begins with glue and paper, not silicon: coated circuit boards and the first logic chips are the steam age's contribution to computing.%n%nThe tutorial's 'Circuit Boards' and 'Time For Some Logic' quests set the pace; MIAO asks for a small batch so your LV transition does not stall on day one.%n%nEvery wire, diode and board you press now is interest paid forward to the LV circuit line.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAUg.name=§2§lThe Steam Backbone
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAUg.desc=GT Steam Reborn's hub array is the backbone this whole pack is named for: a 9x9 tower, 3 to 32 blocks tall, each stack layer carrying 25 storage cells — up to 750 cells in one tower.%n%nCell capacities run from 320 million liters (bronze) through 1.28 billion (steel) to 20.48 billion (overpressure). Drop a hub singularity chip in the controller slot and total capacity multiplies by five; auto-output through the hub hatch moves up to 20,000,000 L/s.%n%nThe hub itself drinks no steam. Build it tall, then hang cache nodes off it like ornaments.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAUw.name=Cache and Drill
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAUw.desc=Two workhorses for the hub: the steam fluid drilling rig and the steam cache node.%n%nThe drill is a 5x6x5 rig that pumps water straight from the ground — 200 to 8,000 L/s depending on tier, drinking 1,000 L/s of steam. The cache node is a 16-million-liter digital tank that auto-outputs at 2,000,000 L/s and binds to the hub for two-way transfer — the cheapest node in the whole GTSR family, no singularity required.%n%nWater in, steam round-trip: your first closed utility loop.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAVA.name=Pressure Rising
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAVA.desc=Railcraft's high-pressure boiler tank is the classic steam upgrade: higher temperature, more steam per tick, and a longer, hungrier warm-up.%n%nIt wants a firebox underneath and a pipe on top for extraction. Keep it well fed — an HP boiler that runs cold is just an expensive decoration.%n%nThis is the last single-block boiler you will need; the next stop is multiblock mirror farms.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAVQ.name=Sun on the Roof
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAVQ.desc=Bronze solar boilers: free steam while the sun is up, no fuel bill at all.%n%nThey only work outdoors, in daylight, under open sky — roof real estate is now an engineering resource. Cluster them above the workshop and let coal handle the night shift.%n%nLater, GTSR will turn this hobby into a 30x27x7 mirror farm. Start collecting roofs now.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAVg.name=Mirror Farm
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAVg.desc=GTSR's large solar superpressure array: a 30x27x7 farm of mirrors, 120,000 L/s bronze, 180,000 steel, 240,000 silver — the silver tier blows straight past ordinary steam into superheated.%n%nSteam-to-water ratio is 160:1, and it only heats by day, outdoors, under clear sky. Solar boosters in the controller slot add up to +200 percent on the solar share; stacked with superpressure mode the ceiling is four times base output.%n%nVoid-world players: this is your power plant. Everyone else: this is your retirement plan.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAVw.name=Magma into Steam
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAVw.desc=GTSR's large geothermal steam boiler drinks lava and exhales steam: 60,000 L/s bronze, 150,000 L/s steel, scaled by heat percentage.%n%nEach cycle burns 1,000 mB of lava with probabilistic souvenirs — obsidian at 20 percent, ash 10, sulfur 8, and friends. Cold start guzzles 333 mB/s; a hot steel boiler settles at 200 mB/s. Superpressure mode (screwdriver, heat at 100 percent) multiplies the ceiling.%n%nNether access turns this from convenience into doctrine.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAWA.name=The Big Melt
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAWA.desc=GTSR's large steam furnace is a 5x5x5 parallel smelter running vanilla furnace recipes: 24 parallel slots in bronze, 48 in steel.%n%nIt applies an EU-cost discount before conversion (bronze 0.6, steel 0.4) and a time modifier (0.4 / 0.2), then bills you in steam. Superheated steam runs it at four times speed and four times thirst.%n%nThe structure only asks for casing, firebox and frame plus one steam input hatch and an output bus. Smelting bottlenecks are now history.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAWQ.name=§c§lBrick by Brick to Steel
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAWQ.desc=The reinforced brick blast furnace is your ticket out of the bronze age: a compact 3x3x5 all-steel multiblock running the GT5U brick-blast recipe table.%n%nController recipe: GT5U brick blast furnace controller plus eight steel plates. Firebrick on top, solid steel casing in the middle, steel combustion chamber at the bottom. Oven heat climbs 0.01 percent per second while running; parallel slots scale from 1 (cold) to 4 (hot) with recipe speed up to 1.5x.%n%nMIAO's steam-segment challenge: bring the furnace §cand§r a serious coal reserve. Steel does not negotiate.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAWg.name=§6§lSteam Age Milestone
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAWg.desc=First milestone of seven. The audit is intentionally classical: boards, logic, and one steel-grade steam machine that proves you own the age.%n%nTurn in a batch of coated circuit boards, a stack of first-generation logic chips, and a steel steam alloy smelter — the machine the tutorial reserves for 'High Speed Alloys'.%n%nReward includes your first §6shimmering neko coin§r. Six more of these wait at LV, MV, HV, EV, IV and LuV. The spine has begun.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAWw.name=§a§lLet There Be EU
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAWw.desc=The LV era turns pressure into electricity. Machine hulls, good circuits, electric macerators — and the first rule of GT law: §cvoltage discipline§r.%n%nThe tutorial's Safety First canon, abbreviated: machines left in the rain start fires; fires next to machines roll dice with explosions; feeding a machine higher voltage than its tier detonates it; excess amps are harmless but overpowered cables melt and burn everything nearby.%n%nThis segment also introduces the MIAO way of wiring: GTSWN link covers, no cables at all.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAXA.name=Hulls Before Machines
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAXA.desc=Every LV machine starts as a machine hull — the tutorial's 'Basic Crafting: Machine Hulls' is the gate you pass here.%n%nFour hulls means four machines' worth of ambition: macerator, alloy smelter, extractor, and whatever else survives the planning meeting.%n%nHull tiers continue all the way to LuV; today's humble LV shells are the first of six collectibles.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAXQ.name=First Electric Grind
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAXQ.desc=The electric macerator replaces steam muscle with quiet, tireless EU — the tutorial's 'Electric Macerator' in your colors now.%n%nFeed it the crushed-ore stream your bronze machines built and watch the dust drawers fill at a pace steam never managed.%n%nKeep it indoors, keep it LV, and it will outlive several of your later engineering decisions.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAXg.name=Smelt, Assemble, Repeat
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAXg.desc=Two machines define the LV workflow: the alloy smelter for metals, the circuit assembler for brains.%n%nThe circuit assembler is where boards, diodes and wire stop being inventory and start being technology. Run it beside the alloy smelter and the LV circuit line feeds itself.%n%nAnnealed copper comes as a bonus — the tutorial's cheaper-annealed trick is worth adopting on day one.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAXw.name=§2§lGood Circuits, Great Circuits
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAXw.desc=The LV circuit: good electronic circuits, the very item the tutorial's 'Good Circuits' quest celebrates.%n%nFour of them unlocks everything LV wants to be — assemblers, extractors and the wireless gear coming right up.%n%nIf diodes are the bottleneck, the gallium arsenide route in the tutorial's Diodes quest is your friend.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAYA.name=§b§lYour First Wireless Tap
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAYA.desc=MIAO's signature LV move: the GTSWN wireless network link terminal. Craft it with 4 LV emitters, 2 steel plates, a computer screen cover and 2 steel screws.%n%nShift+right-click flips between §bEnergy mode§r (draw from the wireless grid) and §bPower mode§r (feed the grid); the texture always shows which is live. Right-click any GT machine to attach a link anchor cover and the machine drinks EU as if cabled.%n%nThe gtswn pack teaches the full toolkit; here we just wire the LV floor the MIAO way.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAYQ.name=Drinking From the Air
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAYQ.desc=Energy mode in practice: with the terminal in Energy mode, right-click a GT machine to attach a Link Anchor (Energy) cover.%n%nThe cover buffers V x A x 800 ticks, injects up to V x A per tick, and refills from the wireless grid every 600 ticks at the configured downlink loss — 15 percent by default. Removing the cover refunds its buffer. Pointing at a machine draws a yellow 3x3 grid highlight.%n%nCheckbox when your first machine runs with zero cables attached.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAYg.name=Feed the Grid, Distantly
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAYg.desc=Power mode in practice: flip the terminal, right-click a generator-type machine, and the Link Anchor (Power) cover becomes a virtual cable — draining output into its buffer and uploading to the wireless grid every 600 ticks.%n%nUplink loss defaults to zero, and the cover blocks real-cable output on that side so nothing is double-counted. The grid highlight turns purple in Power mode.%n%nEvery distant dynamo is now a grid feeder. Your LV boiler room just became infrastructure.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAYw.name=§5§lSteam to EU, Bridged
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAYw.desc=GTSR's giant steam turbine array is the bridge between everything steam and the electric ages: a 13x13 base, 9 to 25 blocks tall, twelve casing tiers from steel to spacetime.%n%nEfficiency caps by steam grade: ordinary 150 percent, superheated 200, supercritical 250, dense supercritical 300 — dense grades need a tier-6-plus body. Steam priority is automatic: dense supercritical first, then supercritical, dense superheated, dense, superheated, ordinary. Condensate flows back as distilled water.%n%nRun your hub's surplus through this and LV pays its own bills.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAZA.name=Voltage Discipline
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAZA.desc=A doctrine quest, no steel required. Recite the Safety First canon until it is reflex: §crain starts fires, fires gamble with explosions, overvoltage detonates machines, cables melt first and take the wall with them§r.%n%nExcess amperage is harmless — voltage is the killer. One transformer mistake at LV is a crater; at LuV it is a monument.%n%nOptional: show a redstone buffer stock, because every safety circuit you will ever build starts with dust.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAZQ.name=Automation Underground
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAZQ.desc=The LV miner: the tutorial's 'Auto Mining' entry, now on MIAO's payroll.%n%nGive it mining pipes, a drill and power, and it eats a chunk column while you do literally anything else. What it lacks in byproduct richness it repays in not being you, underground, with a pickaxe.%n%nPair it with the macerator line and the ore chain is hands-free from bedrock to ingot.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAZg.name=Tanks Fit for a Factory
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAZg.desc=GT tanks, LV edition — the tutorial's 'GT Tanks, the LV Version'. Buckets are for pioneers; a tank wall is for industry.%n%nWater for boilers, creosote from the coke push, distilled water from the turbine condensate — everything the steam age taught you to move now has somewhere to go.%n%nStack them, label them, and stop mopping.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAZw.name=Boiler, LV Refit
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAZw.desc=The LV advanced steam boiler: electric-age controls on steam-age physics, straight from the tutorial's LV boiler quest.%n%nIt sits between your coal-fired past and the turbine future, quietly pressing steam while the LV floor spins up.%n%nFeed it from the tanks you just built; that is what they are for.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAaA.name=§c§lA Stack of Good Circuits
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAaA.desc=MIAO is a challenge pack, so the LV finale costs double: §csixteen§r good circuits, not the tutorial's polite pair.%n%nThat is four assembler batches or one very good afternoon. The reward package of plates and coins is scaled accordingly.%n%nEverything after this — wireless gear, MV hulls, the entire aluminium economy — assumes this stack exists. Make it so.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAaQ.name=§a§lLow Voltage Milestone
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAaQ.desc=Second milestone. The audit: a working stock of LV circuits and the hulls that prove you can channel them.%n%nEight good circuits and two LV machine hulls — small numbers that represent an entire electrical civilization booting up in your base.%n%nSecond §6shimmering neko coin§r earned. MV's aluminium vault is next door.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAag.name=§2§lMedium Voltage, Heavy Alloys
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAag.desc=MV begins with a materials problem: the MV machine hull wants aluminium, and so does every MV machine part after it. The tutorial's advice stands — MV electrolyzer for aluminium, MV extruder for cheaper parts, MV miner for your weekends.%n%nVoltage law gets stricter, not softer: feeding MV to an LV machine explodes it; MV over LV cable melts the cable and burns the crater wider. Higher-tier machines also run lower-tier recipes faster — safely, if you wire it safely.%n%nPlan aluminium stock like ammunition. You will need magazines of it.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAaw.name=The Aluminium Hull
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAaw.desc=The MV machine hull — the tutorial's 'Tier 2 (MV)' milestone item, aluminium through and through.%n%nOne hull unlocks the whole MV single-block catalogue; the catalogue will happily unlock your entire afternoon.%n%nNote the damage-value etiquette: this exact hull is what every MV multiblock energy-hatch story starts from.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAbA.name=Aluminium the Electrolyte Way
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAbA.desc=The MV electrolyzer is aluminium's front door — the tutorial's 'Electrolyzing All the Things' handles bauxite and friends at industrial current.%n%nRun dust in, ingots out, and keep the cell fed: the hull you just claimed has siblings, dozens of them.%n%nBonus choice of redstone or iron to keep the electrode side of your base humming.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAbQ.name=§6§lStainless Ambitions
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAbQ.desc=The Electric Blast Furnace — the tutorial's 'Stainless Taking Too Long?' controller — is the machine MV is secretly about.%n%nStainless steel comes out hot and stays hot; the EBF turns ore-grade ambition into alloy-grade reality. Bring a controller and a working stock of stainless ingots to prove the line runs.%n%nThree levers for speed, per the tutorial: better coils, higher-tier energy hatches, or simply more furnaces. You will pull all three.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAbg.name=Shared Walls, Shared Smoke
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAbg.desc=The tutorial's multiblock etiquette lesson, now with MIAO enforcement: multiblock machines §6can§r share casings, mufflers and input/output hatches and buses — but never maintenance hatches (auto ones excepted).%n%nEnergy hatches can be shared only if you can actually feed every machine they serve. And two EBFs at once means §cpollution you can taste§r: use the best mufflers and think about where the wind blows.%n%nCheckbox it, then go hug your muffler array.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAbw.name=Better Grinding
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAbw.desc=The advanced macerator — 'Advanced Macerator' in the tutorial's MV catalogue — grinds finer, faster and with better manners at MV current.%n%nSlotted into your existing dust line, it quietly doubles throughput without doubling footprint.%n%nThe universal version waits at HV; today's model is just the warm-up act.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAcA.name=First Drops of Distilling
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAcA.desc=The distillery — the oil line's 'Simple Distillation' machine — is your first handshake with petroleum.%n%nCrude in, fractions out; the oil age formally opens the moment this thing hisses. Keep it fed and keep its outputs segregated.%n%nA tower-sized sequel waits at HV, and a super-sized one at EV. Start practicing your fraction bookkeeping now.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAcQ.name=Way Two: The Pyrolyse Oven
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAcQ.desc=The tutorial offers two roads to charcoal; the pyrolyse oven is the one that scales.%n%nLogs in, charcoal and creosote out, all at MV power. Creosote feeds the brick-blast furnace push; charcoal feeds everything else.%n%nChoice of coal or an extra wood bundle, because pyrolyse economics reward hoarders.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAcg.name=§5§l"Rare" Metals
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAcg.desc=The MV alumni set: stainless steel and kanthal. Stainless feeds EBF-grade everything; kanthal is your first serious coil alloy and the tutorial's 'First Kanthal' badge.%n%nSixteen stainless ingots and a starter stack of kanthal prove the EBF and its supply chain both breathe.%n%nThe name says rare; the recipe says logistics. Both are telling the truth.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAcw.name=Steam Meets the Oil Vein
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAcw.desc=GTSR's vein steam pyrolyzer: a 7x7x7 tower that locks onto the fluid under its chunk on first run, then scans neighbors for the same payload.%n%nEach completed cycle (360 seconds bronze / 180 steel) injects +10,000 or +25,000 mB into every matching chunk — an oilfield that refills itself. Steam cost: 1,000 / 4,000 L/s.%n%nThe gtsr pack covers the chip ladder; here it is the MV segment's fluid-megastructure opening move.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAdA.name=Dense Steam Prep
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAdA.desc=The self-powered mechanical steam compressor compresses 4 steam into 1 superheated: bronze eats 6,400 L/s and exhales 1,600 L/s superheated plus 30 L/s distilled water; steel does 25,600 into 6,400 plus 120.%n%nIt runs every tick, checks no recipes, and demands a single-tier structure — bronze and steel parts never mix into one shell.%n%nThis is the superheated plant that will feed the open hearth, the processing array and later the drilling hub. The dense variants it enables are EV's currency.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAdQ.name=The Oven That Learns
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAdQ.desc=GTSR's large coke oven stacks the GT5U coke oven into a 3x7x7 multiblock with a temper: base speed 120 percent bronze, 200 percent steel, plus another +1 percent per percent of oven heat while running.%n%nA hot steel oven cooks a coal in about forty-five seconds. Idle, it cools at one percent per second — so keep it fed, not fashionable.%n%nCoal in, coke and creosote out: the brick-blast furnace's favorite buffet.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAdg.name=Mining Without Wandering
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAdg.desc=The MV miner — 'Auto Mining MV' in the tutorial's catalogue — is the LV model with a promotion.%n%nBetter drills, better pipes, fewer excuses to be underground personally. Park it over a marked ore vein and let geology do the commuting.%n%nIt will hold the line until EV's steam crust rig arrives to make a philosophical statement about mining.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAdw.name=§c§lKanthal by the Bundle
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAdw.desc=MIAO's MV challenge: §cthirty-two§r kanthal ingots — double the tutorial's 'First Kanthal' ask.%n%nKanthal coils are the EBF's mid-game metabolism; HV will want its own furnace running hot from day one. Stock deep or stock twice.%n%nThe stainless rebate in the reward keeps the alloy budget roughly self-funding.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAeA.name=§2§lMedium Voltage Milestone
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAeA.desc=Third milestone. The audit is aluminium-age baroque: good integrated circuits, the MV hull, and sixteen stainless ingots as testimony that the EBF runs like a utility.%n%nBehind these three line items stands an electrolyzer floor, a coil program and a coke oven with a temperament.%n%nThird §6shimmering neko coin§r. HV brings titanium, vacuum cold and your first look at full automation.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAeQ.name=§5§lHigh Voltage, New Rules
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAeQ.desc=HV is where GTNH opens up: titanium asks for vacuum-freezer cold, the AE2 chapter (Zone 2 of this very book) unlocks full automation, and GTSWN's info panels turn telemetry into architecture.%n%nThe tutorial frames HV as the recycling-and-scrap tier too: garbage becomes scrap, scrap becomes UU-amplifier, and matter starts bending to budget.%n%nThis segment audits your automation as seriously as your metallurgy. Both must be real.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAeg.name=Hull Number Three
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAeg.desc=The HV machine hull — the tutorial's 'Tier 3 (HV)' handover item, as usual.%n%nTitanium-frame machines deserve their shell; everything from universal grinders to mass fabricators will live inside one of these.%n%nCollect the whole set: this is hull number three of six.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAew.name=§5§lTitanium, Finally
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAew.desc=The tutorial says it best: you just need to cool those hot ingots down in your Vacuum Freezer and you get your first titanium.%n%nEight ingots certify that the hot-to-cold chain works — EBF on one end, vacuum on the other, logistics between.%n%nTitanium is HV's backbone alloy; every serious multiblock from here to LuV has some in its family tree.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAfA.name=Circuit Assembly, Advanced
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAfA.desc=The advanced circuit assembler — the tutorial's HV-era upgrade — prints the processor families HV is named after.%n%nFeed it wafers, SMDs and patience; take out integrated processors and their increasingly opinionated cousins.%n%nThe EV edition of this machine arrives two segments later. Machines age; the pipeline is forever.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAfQ.name=Universal Grinding
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAfQ.desc=The universal macerator — 'Universal Macerator' in the tutorial's HV catalogue — merges ore grinding into one civilized machine.%n%nMultiple output slots mean byproducts stop being a loss leader; the dust line simplifies into a single, smug box.%n%nCombine with the LV/MV grinders you already own for a grinding orchestra in three voltages.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAfg.name=§9§lThe AE Integration Check
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAfg.desc=HV is where the tutorial hands you the keys to full automation, and Zone 2 of this book teaches the AE2 side. This quest checks the handshake between them.%n%nCraft the GTSWN ME Network Quantum Terminal — 4 fluix blocks on the corners, 4 LV emitters in the cross, 1 ME controller in the center — then right-click a running, formed controller to quantize it. Quantized controllers mine 1000x slower and shrug off explosions.%n%nIf your ME network cannot spare a controller yet, Zone 2's chapter has shopping lists. The spine will wait; excellence should not.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAfw.name=Trends on a Wall
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAfw.desc=The GTSWN network info panel renders grid history as smooth curves on a wall-sized screen: 4 LV sensors on the corners, glass in a cross, one wireless energy monitor in the center — yes, it eats a monitor, plan ahead.%n%nIt tracks 8 nested time windows (5m to 1Y) on a 61-point FIFO with Fritsch-Carlson splines; all screens placed by the same player share one dataset.%n%nHold an item or fluid and right-click to bind AE stock charts too. Your base now has a stock exchange.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAgA.name=Redstone Meets the Panel
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAgA.desc=HV's GTSWN finale: the wireless energy monitor driving redstone. Bring the monitor (the tutorial-grade LV machine, MTE and all) and comparators to wire the logic.%n%nFive modes cycle on its GUI — Off, High Level, Low Level, High-Hysteresis, Low-Hysteresis — with full-strength 15 output. The anchor toggle switches between stored-energy and EU/t measurement; Param1/Param2 accept scientific notation.%n%nEnergy refreshes every 5 seconds; redstone reacts every 2 ticks. Wire the output into your boiler water line and let physics do the night shift.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAgQ.name=Scrap to UU
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAgQ.desc=The tutorial's HV economics: recycle wood, saplings, netherrack and other garbage for scrap (not a guaranteed drop), then fold scrap into UU-amplifier to run UU-matter at a quarter of the time and a fraction of the energy.%n%nCobblestone, stone and tiny dusts are too cheap to count — the recycler ignores them. Everything else is fair game.%n%nThe recycler itself is the machine on trial here. Feed it your mistakes.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAgg.name=Tower of Drops
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAgg.desc=The distillation tower: the oil line's towering sequel to your MV distillery. Controller plus a stack of distillation casings — the more layers, the finer the fractions.%n%nFeed it crude and it hands back the whole petroleum family tree: light and heavy fractions, gases, lubricant feedstock. The old distillery gets to retire to backup duty.%n%nAn EV-grade upgrade path exists; do not get attached to any particular tower height.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAgw.name=Oily Advice
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAgw.desc=A veteran of the old pack left one instruction behind: §6focus on petrochemistry§r. HV is when that advice pays rent.%n%nThe oil cracker turns your tower's heavier output into lighter, more useful chemistry — plastics, rubber and the refined fuels everything else runs on. One cracker, sixteen casings of intent.%n%nBuild the cracker, keep the tower fed, and let the black gold do your homework.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAhA.name=Conduit Cascade
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAhA.desc=EnderIO conduits — the tutorial's 'Conduits' bundle, all three flavors: item, liquid and power, eight apiece.%n%nThey thread through single blocks where GT pipes need hatches and ceremony. Filters and speed upgrades exist when you are ready to get emotional about routing.%n%nZone 4 will lean on this plumbing when the three-mod integration chapter arrives. Lay it generously.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAhQ.name=Ammonia Alley
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAhQ.desc=GTSR's ammonia factory: a 10x14x13 all-steel plant turning refinery gas plus nitrogen into ammonia, with superheated steam as exhaust. Your MV-era atmospheric centrifuge supplies the nitrogen side.%n%nPreheat wants 8,000 L/s steam plus 200 L/s refinery gas; running costs 12,000 L/s steam plus 200 L/s gas, and every parallel slot adds 16,000 L/s divided by reaction seconds.%n%nRecipes demand 100 percent heat — the same warm-up discipline as the open hearth. Catalysts live in the gtsr pack's chapters.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAhg.name=§c§lA Bridge of Titanium
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAhg.desc=MIAO's HV challenge: §cthirty-two§r titanium ingots — double the tutorial's 'Titanium, Finally' ask.%n%nThat is a vacuum freezer running on marital hours and an EBF that considers itself the main character. The reward rebates eight back so the multiblock push can start immediately.%n%nTitanium is not just an alloy here; it is the bridge every HV multiblock crosses. Build the bridge.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAhw.name=§5§lHigh Voltage Milestone
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAhw.desc=Fourth milestone. The audit: integrated processors (the HV circuit), the HV hull, and sixteen titanium ingots as metallurgical collateral.%n%nOne line of dialogue from the tutorial ages well here: 'Your first hull, as usual...' — by now the hull is tradition.%n%nFourth §6shimmering neko coin§r. EV is where MIAO stops politely waiting for the tutorial's permission.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAiA.name=§c§lExtreme Voltage, Big Toys
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAiA.desc=The tutorial frames EV around a tier-2 rocket and Mars, plus a stern note to rethink your power setup as machines keep upgrading. MIAO adds its own tradition: this is where the GTSR singularity chain ignites.%n%nExpect multiblocks with footprints measured in parking lots: processing arrays, open hearths, drilling hubs. Superheated steam stops being a luxury and becomes a utility.%n%nKeep the voltage law close. EV mistakes are visible from orbit.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAiQ.name=Hull Number Four
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAiQ.desc=The EV machine hull — 'Tier 4 (EV)' in the tutorial's ledger, handed over as always with a straight face.%n%nTungstensteel-frame machines start here. If your hull foundry is still running on canthal coils, this is the prompt to upgrade the furnace instead of the hull count.%n%nFour of six. The collection is becoming a personality trait.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAig.name=Workstation Wafers
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAig.desc=The EV circuit — the tutorial crowns it 'Workstations, Your First EV Circuit'. Two of them prove the wafer-to-workstation pipeline breathes.%n%nEV electronics want their own assembler tier, their own boards and their own opinions about cleanliness. The tutorial's 'Empty Epoxid Board' and fiber-reinforced lines are the supply-side homework.%n%nDeliver the pair; keep the production line — it has four more generations to feed.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAiw.name=§8§lTungstensteel Tide
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAiw.desc=Tungstensteel — 'needed in all machines for the next tier', says the tutorial, and it means it. Sixteen ingots is the entry ticket; the real order of magnitude comes later.%n%nThe alloy chain wants tungsten, steel and patience, usually through the mixed-oxide route your HV chemistry built.%n%nA rebate of eight keeps the first EV machines from starving while the tide comes in.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAjA.name=Circuit Assembly, EV Edition
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAjA.desc=The EV circuit assembler — the tutorial's dedicated machine for the workstation generation.%n%nSame job as ever, more decimals: boards and components in, EV circuits out, at a voltage that forgives nothing.%n%nThis closes the assembler trilogy for the spine; the IV and LuV editions are victory laps, not obstacles.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAjQ.name=§6§lIgnite the Singularity Chain
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAjQ.desc=MIAO's EV tradition begins: the GTSR steam singularity entangler — the SSE — is the factory that turns endless steam into singularity after singularity.%n%nIt is a 24x30x23 steel structure that swallows the highest grade of ordinary steam in its input hatches every second. Heat accumulates as coefficient x 0.005 x x/(x+200,000) per cycle — coefficients 0.5 / 1.0 / 2.0 for ordinary / superheated / supercritical. At 100 percent heat it resets and produces exactly one steam-entangled singularity.%n%nNo power, no coolant, no mercy. And remember: dropped singularities detonate after 5 seconds — bag them, do not decorate with them. Zone 4 runs this chain into an economy; here we only light the fire.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAjg.name=An Array for Everything
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAjg.desc=GTSR's dynamic processing array: a 7x5x7 chassis that eats GT single-block machines whole. Drop an electrolyzer, centrifuge, chemical reactor, mixer, packer, brewery or rock crusher into the controller slot and the array runs that machine's recipe table at multiblock scale.%n%nIt takes superheated steam only. Parallelism is (1 + 2 x machine tier) + stack count, and higher-tier machines sip less steam per recipe — HV 1.0x, IV 0.5x, LuV 0.1x. Base bonus: 200 percent speed.%n%nOne chassis, any recipe. The tutorial's parallel-machine dream, drawn in steel.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAjw.name=Open Hearth, Open Throttle
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAjw.desc=GTSR's open hearth furnace — the Siemens-Martin heart of the steam steel industry: a 12x19x14 giant turning iron and carbon into steel, 2 iron + 2 coal into 2 steel as the base recipe.%n%nIt demands superheated steam — 6,000 L/s during warm-up, easing to 3,000 hot and 1,500 at 200 percent oven heat. Parallelism climbs from 64 to 128 with heat. Coke recipes (1,200s) and carbon dust recipes (800s, fastest) fill the book.%n%nNamed after a furnace design that filled nineteenth-century skies. Yours will fill a twenty-first-century spreadsheet.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAkA.name=Drill Across Dimensions
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAkA.desc=GTSR's singularity drilling hub: a 12x12x12 steel cube that commands remote work nodes — singularity mining nodes dig, drilling nodes pump, and everything lands in the hub's buses and output hatches.%n%nIt insists on superheated steam — 8,000 L/s base plus a tiered draw per active work node — and superheated grants no acceleration. The assembler-controller recipe costs sixteen singularities.%n%nZone 4 wires this into cross-dimensional logistics. On the spine, it is proof that EV steam can reach anywhere.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAkQ.name=Super Distillation
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAkQ.desc=The oil line's 'Super Distillation' machine: the distillation tower's EV-era cousin, taller in spirit and throughput.%n%nBy now your crude pipeline should flow like a utility: pumps in, tower and cracker in chorus, products auto-sorted by the AE2 side of the house.%n%nThe old quest book's advice still stands — focus on petrochemistry — and by EV it pays compounding interest.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAkg.name=Steam-Powered Mining Rig
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAkg.desc=GTSR's crust steam driller — the budget void miner: 9x11x9, running on 2,000 L/s of steam, pulling one random ore from the current dimension's drop table every 500-tick cycle (25 seconds).%n%nIt works in the Overworld and the Nether only; other dimensions refuse to start. Superheated steam halves the cycle to 125 ticks.%n%nIt is the little brother of the crust matter aggregator — same idea, zero singularities. And heed the old EV lesson: plan the power before the fleet, or the fleet plans you.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAkw.name=Water Works, Hub Edition
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAkw.desc=GTSR's water works hub array: the universal fluid hub — 7x7 base, 2 to 31 blocks tall, nine storage slots per layer. It accepts exactly one fluid type at a time; injection locks the type.%n%nCell capacities run 1.28 million liters bronze, 5.12 million steel, 20.48 million tungstensteel; the singularity chip multiplies by five. Auto-output moves 1,280,000 L/s, and bound fluid nodes interact at their own rates.%n%nAnchor it under the turbine condensate loop and distilled water stops being a chore.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAlA.name=§c§lNano Circuits by the Dozen
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAlA.desc=MIAO's EV challenge: §csixteen§r EV workstation circuits — the tutorial's Workstations quest asks for four, and it was not joking either.%n%nThis is the spine's most repetitive gauntlet: boards, SMDs, wafers, assembler time, and a base that hums at all hours. The tungstensteel rebate softens the material edge.%n%nZone 4's integration chapter will spend EV circuits like coins. Mint them.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAlQ.name=More Power, Sir
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAlQ.desc=The tutorial's speed advice, EV edition: better coils, higher-tier energy hatches, or more machines. Two EV energy hatches prove you chose door number two.%n%nHigher-tier hatches let multiblocks overclock without re-plumbing the whole power room — the polite way to scale.%n%nA stack of redstone blocks comes with, because every control circuit you sketch tonight starts the same way.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAlg.name=§c§lExtreme Voltage Milestone
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAlg.desc=Fifth milestone. The audit: four EV workstation circuits, the EV hull, and sixteen tungstensteel ingots.%n%nEV was where MIAO asked you to go big — processing arrays, open hearths, a singularity chain with its pilot light on. The audit trusts you kept them lit.%n%nFifth §6shimmering neko coin§r. Two segments remain: the insane, and the luminous.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAlw.name=§d§lInsane Voltage
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAlw.desc=The tutorial's IV briefing, verbatim in spirit: get a set of TPV coils for your EBF, have an Alloy Blast Smelter ready, and visit Mars, Phobos and Deimos. Also — 'you should've learned how to GregTech by now; if not, go clean up the machine craters in your base.'%n%nMIAO's IV is short and sharp: the GTSR critical singularity stabilizer and the dense-steam overpressure system. Five quests, no filler.%n%nThe naquadah line starts here too — the tutorial calls its full processing too complex for IV, and offers the EBF shortcut: smelt NOM dust into naquadah nuggets, byproducts optional.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAmA.name=Hull and Assembler, IV
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAmA.desc=The IV pair: machine hull and circuit assembler, the tutorial's 'Tier 5 (IV)' and 'IV Circuit Assembler' in one sitting.%n%nTungstensteel shells, mainframe-grade circuits, and the quiet confidence of a base that no longer photographs its craters for insurance.%n%nA choice of lootbag or diamonds, because IV taste is personal.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAmQ.name=Critical Singularity, Carefully
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAmQ.desc=GTSR's critical singularity stabilizer — the CSC, the SSE's big sibling: a 27x27x21 rhodium-palladium body that accepts dense variants only. Dense steam, dense superheated, dense supercritical.%n%nIts saturation curve is far friendlier: 0.005 x x/(x+5,000) per cycle, so a modest dense-steam stream fills the bar much faster than the SSE ever dreamed. At 100 percent heat: one critical steam-entangled singularity.%n%nHandle with respect — a dropped critical singularity detonates after §c3 seconds§r. Zone 4 turns these into industrial policy; on the spine, one machine is testimony enough.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAmg.name=The Overpressure Club
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAmg.desc=The dense-state steam manipulator: a 21x30x11 tier-2 rhodium-palladium body that compresses ordinary steam into dense at 1000:1 — and decompresses back at 1:1000 with a screwdriver flip.%n%nIt eats whatever grade sits in its input hatches — steam, superheated or supercritical — and accumulates the matching dense variant at real-number rates. This is the pressure economy's central bank.%n%nPair it with overpressure hub cells (20.48 billion liters each) and your steam accounting becomes astronomy. The gtsr pack keeps the deep lore; the spine just needs the machine standing.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAmw.name=§d§lInsane Voltage Milestone
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAmw.desc=Sixth milestone. The audit: one mainframe circuit — the tutorial's 'Mainframes, Your First IV Circuit' — the IV hull, and four naquadah ingots as proof the NOM shortcut is running.%n%nIV was five quests of pressure and composure. The spine's final act waits one segment over.%n%nSixth §6shimmering neko coin§r. One to go.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAnA.name=§b§lLuV: Light the Way
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAnA.desc=The tutorial's LuV briefing, honored in full: machine parts now want rhodium-plated palladium, the Assembly Line becomes mandatory for high-tier components, and the suggested destinations are Io, Venus and Mercury.%n%nThe crystal circuit family lives here — chips, processors, mainframes — with the tutorial noting the Crystal Mainframe is the first UV circuit you can make. Overachievement is a LuV tradition.%n%nThis final spine segment is five quests long, and then the rest of the book takes over: deep integration, challenges, and the exchange counter.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAnQ.name=The Assembly Line
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAnQ.desc=The Assembly Line — the tutorial's Multiblock Goals centerpiece: five to sixteen blocks long, minimum length set by the recipe's component and fluid count.%n%nFrom LuV onward, motors, pistons, robot arms and pumps graduate here; the single-block assembler becomes a museum piece with excellent signage.%n%nGlass, conveyors, frames and robot-arm casings in quantity. It is less a machine than a commitment ceremony.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAng.name=Crystal Processors
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAng.desc=The LuV circuit hand: crystal processors from the crystal chip line, printed on the LuV circuit assembler you bring alongside.%n%nCrystal chips grow, get doped, get cut; processors assemble; mainframes loom one step after. The tutorial rates the whole family as comfortably over-engineered for LuV — which is exactly why we like it.%n%nTwo processors and the machine that makes them: the spine's last hardware delivery.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAnw.name=The Road Ahead
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAnw.desc=The spine ends at the next quest, but the book does not. Three doors stand open from here.%n%n§6Zone 4§r (the Integration Gate at seq 161) runs GTSR, GTSWN and GTIT as one industrial ecosystem — singularity economies, wireless coverage at scale, and neko-coin commerce. §cZone 5§r (the Challenge Gate at seq 206) collects the bee line, the jump-tier gear and other expensive habits. §dZone 6§r (the Exchange Gate at seq 231) is where shimmering coins go to die happy — and where the MIAO-GTNH Master waits at seq 250.%n%nTake the preview checkbox. The spine carried you this far; the rest of the map is yours.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAoA.name=§d§lLuV Milestone
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAoA.desc=Seventh and final milestone of the spine. The audit: the LuV machine hull, eight naquadah-alloy ingots, and one crystal mainframe — the tutorial's note that it doubles as your first UV circuit is the LuV spirit in a single item.%n%nBehind you: seven ages, seven shimmering coins, a GTSR pressure economy, a GTSWN grid without cables, and a GTIT wallet that has seen things. Ahead: Zone 4's integration, Zone 5's challenges, Zone 6's counter, and the Master at seq 250 watching all of it.%n%nSeventh §6shimmering neko coin§r — the complete set. The spine stands. Go be a problem for the endgame.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAoQ.name=§a§lGate of Crossover
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAoQ.desc=MV is behind you, and the three MIAO mods stop being side shows from here on. This zone weaves GT Steam Reborn's steam industry, GT Simple Wireless Network's cable-free EU grid, and GT Interesting Thing's cat economy into one main road: hubs feed machines, wireless moves the watts, cats pay the bills.%n%nThree strands leave this gate and converge again at the MIAO Industrial Complex at the far end. Every strand sits on the main prereq chain — skipping a mod is not an option.%n%nOld friends return upgraded: the hub network, the singularity chain, the wireless toolkit and the ring collection all live here in crossover form.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAog.name=§6§lSteam Feeds the Line
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAog.desc=Stop dragging steam pipes across the map. The Steam Hub Array is a 9x9 tower, 3 to 32 blocks tall: every layer adds 25 storage slots, up to 750 units. A plain unit holds 320M liters, reinforced 1.28G — and the Hub Singularity Chip multiplies the whole tank by 5 and unlocks node transfer.%n%nWire it into your MV line through steam hub output hatches: auto-output runs at 20,000,000 L/s, more steam than any GT machine row can drink. Your bill is recipe math, not pipe math: steam (L/s) = EUt x 10,000 / max(1,000, efficiency).%n%nHand in a formed controller and its chip to open the crossover road.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAow.name=The Pipe That Isn't
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAow.desc=Cache nodes replace fluid pipes entirely. A Steam Cache Node is a 16,000,000 L digital tank that auto-outputs 2,000,000 L/s through the face it points at; the Reinforced grade quadruples to 64,000,000 L and 8,000,000 L/s and binds to hubs for two-way transfer at the same rate.%n%nThe upgrade path is crafting-table cheap: triple bronze plates and large bronze pipes around a ULV fluid tank core, then triple steel around your existing node. Binding costs 0 singularities plain, 1 reinforced — the friendliest bill in the family.%n%nThen build the void reservoir, the old dream: a Water Hub Array with fluid cache nodes capped over your water works — nodes of 2M / 8M / 32M liters interacting at 64K-2M per cycle, all binding for zero singularities. The tank farm retires, and the boilers never calcify on distilled again.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAApA.name=§2§lOne Ticket, Anywhere
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAApA.desc=The Hub Terminal is the remote control for the whole network: one singularity ringed by eight steel plates in a crafting table. Right-click any hub controller for a status screen listing every bound node with rate, capacity, mode and rename controls.%n%nThe teleport button is the crossover trick: one Steam Entangled Singularity moves you to that node — cross-dimension included, with automatic chunk loading and a safe-spot search. The coin is charged only after a landing spot is found; no safe spot, no charge. You must stand within 8 blocks of the controller when clicking.%n%nRight-click a node to cycle its transfer rate, sneak-right-click to flip input/output mode.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAApQ.name=§c§lFirst Entangler
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAApQ.desc=Stop looting chests for singularities — print them. The Steam Singularity Entangler is a 24x30x23 steel structure that swallows the highest grade of plain steam in its input hatches once per second, all of it.%n%nHeat accumulates as coefficient x 0.005 x x/(x+200,000) per cycle, where x is the liters eaten this cycle; coefficients are 0.5 plain / 1.0 superheated / 2.0 supercritical. At 100%% heat it resets and drops exactly 1 Steam Entangled Singularity. No EU, no cooling hatch.%n%nIdle machines bleed 1%% heat per second — keep it fed, and keep the output bus clear so production never pauses.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAApg.name=Economy of Scale
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAApg.desc=One entangler is a curio; a row of them is a mint. On plain steam at 80,000 L/s each singularity costs about 400,000,000 L of steam and 5,000 seconds of patience; superheated steam cuts the clock to 1,250 seconds at 320,000 L/s.%n%nThe crossover thresholds are 8 / 16 / 32 singularities on hand — enough to bind nodes, buy chips and still feed the DSM later. Show a stack of 32 to the quest book.%n%nRemember the saturation curve: past 200,000 L per cycle, extra steam buys less and less heat.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAApw.name=§c§lA Thousand to One
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAApw.desc=The Dense State Manipulator is the bridge to the critical era: a 21x30x11 tier-2 rhodium-plated palladium build that compresses plain steam into dense steam at 1000:1 — and decompresses back at 1:1000 with a screwdriver click.%n%nIt eats whichever grade sits in its hatches — steam, superheated or supercritical — and emits the matching dense variant. Fuel comes from the input bus: a plain singularity lasts 200 seconds but takes a 20%% output haircut; a critical one lasts the same 200 seconds with zero loss.%n%nFeed it criticals once the CSC is online. At million-liter scale that 20%% eats fast.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAqA.name=§c§lCritical Mass
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAqA.desc=The Critical Singularity Compressor is the entangler's big sibling: 27x27x21 of rhodium-plated palladium, and it only accepts dense variants — dense steam, dense superheated, dense supercritical.%n%nIts saturation curve is far friendlier: 0.005 x x/(x+5,000) per cycle. A modest dense stream from the DSM fills the bar dramatically faster than any plain-steam diet ever could. At 100%% heat: exactly 1 Critical Steam Entangled Singularity.%n%nBuild it next to the DSM and keep the dense line short — every liter of dense steam is a thousand liters of plain steam in disguise.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAqQ.name=§c§lHandle With Respect
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAqQ.desc=Hold the hard currency of the endgame. Critical singularities buy the biggest toys: the Cycle Overlimit Chip bills 64 of them, the reinforced hub chip is priced in stacks, DSM fuel runs lossless, and turbine singularity mode 2 multiplies output by 5.%n%nMind the fuse: 3 seconds after touching the ground a critical detonates — 50%% TNT block damage, 20x TNT entity damage — and scatters 4-12 plain singularities on their own 4-10 second fuses. Plains pop after 5 seconds too, for 2x TNT entity damage.%n%nInventory or machines, never the floor.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAqg.name=White-Glove Courier
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAqg.desc=Move a critical singularity from its CSC to a machine that consumes one — without it ever touching the ground as an item. Direct inventory-to-machine transfers only: no droppers, no water streams, no death-drop courier runs.%n%nThe rules again: 3-second fuse on landing, 50%% TNT block damage, 20x entity damage, plus 4-12 scattered plains on shorter fuses. A dropped critical is a crater with a receipt.%n%nThe quest book trusts your honor. The crater will not.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAqw.name=Overpressure License
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAqw.desc=The tier-3 license: the Reinforced Hub Singularity Chip — node binding, dense and supercritical compatibility, x20 capacity, overpressure node binding. It only works on tungsten steel hubs; lower tiers just show a red warning.%n%nThe bill is endgame-grade: 384 singularities (six stacks), dense europium and rhodium-plated palladium plates, soldered in radon at LuV. The overpressure gear itself is no cheaper — since v1.10.84 an overpressure steam cache node costs 16 critical singularities plus a non-consumed programming circuit.%n%nCapacity check: reinforced units hold 1.28G liters; x20 on a full overpressure build is 10.24 trillion.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAArA.name=Magma Powered
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAArA.desc=The Large Geothermal Steam Boiler drinks lava and breathes steam: 60,000 L/s on bronze, 150,000 L/s on steel, scaling with heat percentage. Every 1,000mB of lava can precipitate obsidian at 20%%, ash at 10%%, sulfur dust at 8%% and more — your lava lake is a strip mine.%n%nDrop a Geothermal Overheat Chip in the controller slot (steel tier only) and the output flips to superheated steam — the 4x speed fuel for every steam-base multiblock. The chip is an LV assembler job: 6 singularities, 4 gold plates, 4 steel plates, 2 LV circuits, 8 LV pumps.%n%nOverpressure mode via screwdriver doubles the heat ceiling to 200%%.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAArQ.name=Refilling the Field
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAArQ.desc=The Vein Steam Pyrolyzer is a 7x7x7 tower that plugs straight into the underground oil system: on first run it locks onto the fluid beneath its chunk, then scans neighbors for the same fluid. Every completed cycle — 360s bronze / 180s steel — it injects +10,000mB / +25,000mB into every matched chunk's reservoir. Oil fields that refill themselves.%n%nRange chips widen the scan: T1 turns 1 chunk into a 2x2, T2 a 4x4, T3 an 8x8 — costing 16 / 32 / 64 singularities up the chain. Steam: 1,000 L/s bronze, 4,000 L/s steel.%n%nA T3 pyrolyzer blankets a small ocean of oil chunks. Feed your ammonia plant forever.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAArg.name=§b§lThe Remote Mine
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAArg.desc=The Singularity Drilling Hub is a 12x12x12 steel cube that drives worker nodes across chunks: miner nodes dig ores, drilling nodes pull underground fluids — everything lands in the hub's own buses and hatches. Its controller recipe bills 16 singularities; binding a worker costs 1 more.%n%nThe steam bill scales with headcount: 8,000 L/s of superheated base plus each active node's tier cost — drillers 2K/6K/12K/20K, miners 5K/10K/20K/80K/240K by tier. Idle nodes cost nothing, and the hub gets no 4x speedup from superheated — it simply demands it.%n%nGrow the network node by node: one miner first, then a driller, then upgrades. Bound nodes chunk-load themselves and their work area.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAArw.name=Matter From Thin Air
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAArw.desc=Two void miners for the price of one road. The Crust Steam Borer is the budget edition: 9x11x9, 2,000 L/s of steam, one 500-tick cycle per random ore from the current dimension's drop table — superheated steam quarters it to 125 ticks. Overworld and Nether only.%n%nThe Crust Matter Aggregator is the singularity edition: 26x26x8, fed by a runaway singularity node, 24,000 L/s of any of three grades — or just 240 L/s of dense. Ore modes 0/1/2 (raw / fortune-rolled rich / crushed at 1.5x), fortune III through XV, and dimension display items to mine other worlds from your living room.%n%nConfigure everything from the terminal GUI — screwdriver-free.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAsA.name=§4§lOne Roof, Whole Chain
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAsA.desc=The Steam Mineral Logistics Cluster closes the crossover road: a 15x12x29 main hall (plus 8-deep extension segments) hosting one controller, seven processing units — crusher, ore washer, centrifuge, thermal centrifuge, sifter, magnetic separator, furnace — five amplifiers and a logistics unit. Ore in, products out, whole chains atomic.%n%nThe bill: 2,000 L/s steam + 10 L/s lubricant to preheat, then 8,000 x 1.5^(chain length - 1) L/s per processing chain. Amplifiers multiply it — parallel x1.3, speed x1.4, primary x2.0, secondary x1.6, steam saver x1.1 — with primary boosts taking only the highest, secondary boosts adding up, and steam savings capped at 48%% total.%n%nBolt auto-maintenance hatches into the structure and say goodbye to wrench duty. This hall is where Z3's EV and IV lines come to be fed.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAsQ.name=§e§lCut the First Cable
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAsQ.desc=The crossover to the wireless strand. Take the Wireless Network Link Terminal, flip it to Energy mode, and right-click your GT machines to attach Link Anchor (Energy) covers — the machines then drink from the wireless EU network like they were cabled in.%n%nThe cover keeps a capacity buffer of V x A x 800 ticks, injects up to V x A per tick, and refills from the wireless grid every 600 ticks with the configured downlink loss — 15 percent by default. Point at a machine to see the 3x3 yellow grid highlight.%n%nStart with the LV room: your steam-fed machines from the hub quest are the first customers. Uplink comes later — for now, someone still has to feed the grid the old-fashioned way.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAsg.name=Meter Maids
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAsg.desc=The Wireless Energy Monitor watches the grid and computes its power with smart statistics. Craft it with 4 LV Sensors, 2 Steel Plates, an LV Machine Hull and 2 Steel Screws — an LV machine that neither consumes nor produces EU. It only measures.%n%nKeep its chunk loaded: a short reload (10 seconds or less) auto-resumes, while a long outage arms a 30 second redstone buffer. Energy refreshes every 5 seconds over a 300 second window.%n%nHang one over the MV array you just wireless-ified, and read the EU/t you are actually pulling through the covers.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAsw.name=Two-Way Street
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAsw.desc=Flip the terminal to Power mode and right-click a generator-type machine to attach a Link Anchor (Power) cover. It acts as a virtual cable: the machine's output drains into an internal buffer and uploads to the wireless grid every 600 ticks.%n%nUplink loss defaults to zero, and the cover blocks the machine from outputting into real cables on that side, so nothing is counted twice. Machines that do not output are skipped automatically. The grid highlight turns purple in Power mode.%n%nThe HV scenario: mega hardware on one side, wireless covers on the other, and the ledger finally balances both ways.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAtA.name=Pocket Patrol
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAtA.desc=The Portable Wireless Network Monitor puts the grid in your pocket. First right-click binds it to you; further right-clicks cycle the HUD Off, Normal and Scientific. It works from any Baubles accessory slot and shows live EU, EU/t rate and a GT-style power tier.%n%nThe gtswn line taught you how to get the toolkit; here the angle is patrol — walk the crossover complex and watch each hall's draw move as machines spin up.%n%nSneak-use it to rebind it to a teammate before a joint shift.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAtQ.name=Watchdog Watts
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAtQ.desc=Redstone time. Place the monitor, give it LV power, right-click the GUI: the redstone mode button cycles Off, High Level, Low Level, High-Hysteresis and Low-Hysteresis, all at full strength 15. The anchor toggle switches what is measured — wireless storage or EU/t status.%n%nRedstone reacts every 2 ticks; energy statistics refresh every 5 seconds over a 300 second window. Wire the output into a comparator circuit and let the grid guard itself: storage below Param1 cuts the feed, EU/t above Param1 lights the alarm.%n%nThat is the old monitor-logic quest, rebuilt as grid security for the crossover complex.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAtg.name=Hysteresis Discipline
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAtg.desc=The two hysteresis modes are where monitoring becomes logic. High-Hysteresis outputs above Param1 and only cancels below Param2; Low-Hysteresis mirrors it. Param1 and Param2 are typed directly — scientific notation like 1.5E6 works.%n%nThe classic build: a High-Hysteresis monitor watching wireless storage trips a steam valve at 90 percent and re-arms at 75 — no flicker, no chatter, no thrashed turbines. A long outage arms a 30 second redstone buffer so a chunk reload never false-trips the logic.%n%nBuild one hysteresis guard around your biggest consumer and report in.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAtw.name=Wall of Trends
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAtw.desc=The Network Info Panel renders grid history as smooth curves on a wall-sized multiblock screen: 4 LV Sensors on the corners, glass in a cross, one Wireless Energy Monitor in the center — yes, it eats a monitor, plan ahead.%n%nIt tracks 8 nested time windows — 5 minutes, 1 hour, 8 hours, 24 hours, 7 days, 1 month, 3 months, 1 year — on a 61-point FIFO, drawing Fritsch-Carlson splines. All screens placed by the same player share one dataset.%n%nIn the crossover complex this is the control-room wall: steam production on one panel, wireless draw on the next.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAuA.name=The Bigger Picture
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAuA.desc=Extender panels stretch the screen into any contiguous rectangle: one craft yields 2 extenders — 4 LV Sensors on the corners, glass cross, a Computer Screen cover in the middle. Extenders auto-attach to adjacent main panels for one continuous display.%n%nHold an item or fluid and right-click the panel to bind an AE stock chart, or add entries to the realtime AE monitor list. The same 8 time windows apply — your ME stock levels ride the same wall as your EU curves.%n%nFour extenders make a proper control-room wall. Craft them and hang the complex's vitals on it.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAuQ.name=Feeling the Void
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAuQ.desc=Point the wireless layer at the power layer: park a portable monitor in your hotbar and stand in the capacitor-bank room while the info walls light up with the reserve's pulse. The old ritual quest — sensing energy from the void — returns as grid-awareness training.%n%nWhat to watch: the 5 second energy refresh over the 300 second window catches brownouts long before your machines stall; the EU/t anchor mode turns the same monitor into a throughput meter for the bank.%n%nNo new hardware beyond the monitor you already own — just a shift of perspective. And keep those chunks loaded.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAug.name=§d§lQuantum Homestead
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAug.desc=The ME Network Quantum Terminal turns a formed ME controller into a remote-access anchor: 4 Fluix Blocks on the corners, 4 LV Emitters in the cross, 1 ME Controller in the center. Right-click a running controller to quantize and bind the whole structure.%n%nWith the bound terminal, right-click any ground to plant Quantum Nodes — no recipe, no cost. Each node bridges adjacent AE2 devices into the network with dense-cable capacity: 32 channels per connection, 10 AE/t idle, vanilla distance limits void. Same dimension only.%n%nChannel discipline: capacity is (faces x 6 minus shared faces) x 32. At 95 percent the GUI warns you; past 100 percent the controller detonates. Nobody scrapes quantized controller off the ceiling. A quantized controller also hardens — 1000x mining time, explosion-proof.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAuw.name=Mass Wireless
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAuw.desc=Scale the cover fleet: eight Link Anchor (Energy) covers on the machine rows, eight Link Anchor (Power) covers on the generator rows. The two-way ledger is simple — downlink costs 15 percent every 600-tick refill, uplink costs nothing, and each cover's buffer is V x A x 800 ticks deep.%n%nThe engineering trick is tiering: put covers on the big HV hardware first, let their fat buffers absorb the refill gaps, and leave the small machines for last. Remember the Power cover blocks cable output on its side — one machine, one side, no double counting.%n%nSixteen covers later, your crossover complex officially has no cables left to cut.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAvA.name=The Machine Ledger
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAvA.desc=The Device Info Terminal has no recipe — it is granted here, as the wireless strand's milestone. Right-click any working GT machine to bind it; right-click air to open the ledger: tri-state rows (Running/Idle/Stopped), instant EU/t, a 60-point rolling average, position and current recipe, five sortable columns.%n%nThe row's sparkle button teleports you to the machine for XP levels; Ctrl+click unbinds remotely; shift+right-click air runs a team-wide scan with a 20 second countdown. Carrying the terminal auto-binds machines you place.%n%nBind the whole crossover complex — the ledger is the mission-control view the walls cannot give you.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAvQ.name=§6§lThe Daily Grind
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAvQ.desc=The economy strand starts with the machine itself — a 2x2x1 Neko Vending Machine, controller bottom-right — and its oldest faucet: the Sign-In tab. One click per day pays 10 coins on weekdays and 20 on weekends; consecutive-day tiers pay out once per month per tier, and cumulative-day tiers pay once per tier, forever.%n%nThe crossover angle: this is not idle trivia — coins are the budget for the shopping quests down this road. A missed day is a missed amplifier.%n%nSign in, then keep the streak. Data survives relogs; admins can check with /gtit signin info.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAvg.name=Paid to Wait
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAvg.desc=The online-time tiers: 30 minutes pays 5 coins, 2 hours pays 20, 5 hours pays 50 — each once per day, and time only counts while you are actually in the server.%n%nHere is the industrial trick nobody tells you: those five hours are exactly when your entangler bank needs supervision, your cluster needs feeding and your drilling nodes need their hubs emptied. AFK where the machines are.%n%nBetween sign-in, streaks and online time, the crossover budget funds itself.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAvw.name=Pity the Gambler
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAvw.desc=The lottery is a system, not a button. Two default pools: Neko at 5 coins a draw, Shimmering at 1 shimmering coin a draw. From 30 failed draws, soft pity adds +5 percent weight per extra draw to high-rarity entries; hard pity forces EPIC-or-better at 50 draws on the Neko pool, 30 on the Shimmering pool.%n%nThe counter only resets when you actually hit EPIC or above — and it is counted per team per pool, so one member grinding the wheel moves the guarantee closer for everyone.%n%nDraw with the crossover budget, not against it.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAwA.name=Sixty-Four Coins
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAwA.desc=Show a full stack of Neko Coins — sixty-four of them, the exact bank rate for one shimmering. Sign-in days, online tiers, lottery wins and vending sales all pooled into one wallet: this is the crossover fund taking shape.%n%nCoins are counted in the wallet, not as inventory clutter — import them at the machine and watch the balance row tick. Team players share one team wallet, so the fund is a group effort.%n%nThe milestone is also a promise: the next quest turns this stack into hard currency.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAwQ.name=The Bank Rate
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAwQ.desc=The exchange counter: 64 Neko Coins buy 1 Shimmering Neko Coin, no cooldown, unlimited volume. The reverse direction buys back at 56 — the 8-coin spread keeps both markets honest.%n%nEvery premium price in the economy is quoted in shimmering: the infinite cells, the shop's top shelf, the lottery's second pool. Knowing the rate tells you what anything costs in sign-in days.%n%nMake one exchange and the hard-currency account is open.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAwg.name=Shopping List
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAwg.desc=First crossover purchase: pay 32 Neko Coins at the vending machine's coin counter and take delivery of link-hardware materials — the redstone and iron that wireless covers and monitors eat by the stack.%n%nThe mechanics: drop the coins into the input slots, press import, and the balance row ticks down as the shop pays out. Prices sit next to their cooldowns; the coin counter never runs dry of the basics.%n%nThe 32 coins handed in here are consumed — this is a purchase, not a donation.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAww.name=Ten Fingers, Ten Rings
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAww.desc=The ring collection, crossover edition. Rings live in Baubles ring slots, and most stack their effects when worn in multiples — the old dream of ten decorated fingers still stands.%n%nStart industrial: the Ring of Distant Grasp adds +2 blocks of interaction and attack range per ring — reach the back row of a machine hall without leaving the walkway. The Ring of Skywalk raises step height to a full block — single-block ledges walk themselves.%n%nHand in one of each; the rest of the collection gets its own trials next door.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAxA.name=Fireproof Commute
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAxA.desc=Two rings that change how you run the industrial complex. The Ring of Dragon's Breath maintains Fire Resistance, Night Vision, Regeneration I and Resistance I — each refresh 30 seconds, renewed every 5, the aura never flickers. Between that and a lava suit, the Nether becomes a commute — which matters when your geothermal boilers need lava by the tank.%n%nThe Ring of Mountainbreaker carries Strength II and Haste II on the same aura — permanent, potion-free mining power for clearing the halls your multiblocks demand.%n%nHand in one of each and wear them to work.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAxQ.name=Bottomless Bucket
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAxQ.desc=The ME Infinity Fluid Cell slots into an ME drive and effectively never fills — adapted from AE2Things, sold at the vending machine's premium counter for shimmering coins.%n%nIn the crossover complex it is the coolant answer: distilled water loops, lubricant for the cluster's preheat, creosote from the coke hall — one cell per fluid and the tank farm retires. Pure storage QoL, no fluid generation.%n%nThe old price was 256 buckets and 64 glass; the premium counter now quotes shimmering. Take delivery.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAxg.name=§5§lBottomless Bin
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAxg.desc=The item twin: the ME Infinity Cell, another AE2 drive resident whose bar refuses to budge. Slot it in next to the fluid cell and your ME network officially stops caring about storage.%n%nThis is where the AE chapter from earlier pays off — the network you built back there now carries an infinite vault. One cell holds everything the crossover complex produces; a second holds everything it consumes.%n%nThe premium counter sells it for shimmering coins. You know the rate by heart now.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAxw.name=Paying Premium
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAxw.desc=Second crossover purchase, hard currency edition: 2 shimmering coins at the premium counter buy a stack of GTSR-grade materials — the pressed plates and circuit stock that hub chips and node upgrades eat.%n%nThe premium counter charges the wallet directly, no item fumbling; prices there are the top of the economy. Two shimmering is 128 sign-in coins — the crossover fund finally flexing.%n%nThe coins handed in are consumed. Shop like you mean it.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAyA.name=The Long Game
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAyA.desc=The economy strand's view of the endgame: you now sign in out of habit, the wallet funds the shop runs, and the rings and cells are on the shelf. The crossover complex is one convergence quest away from finished.%n%nLook ahead: the Gate of Trials in the challenge zone will demand banked criticals and sustained 300 percent engineering; the exchange zone beyond it will happily take your surplus; and the final Master gates on every zone entrance — including this one.%n%nWarm up the economy now. The trials bill in singularities, and singularities bill in steam.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAyQ.name=Triple Redundancy
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAyQ.desc=First convergence quest — steam logistics meets wireless telemetry. Carry the Hub Terminal in one hand, park a Network Info Panel on the wall, and bind the panel to the same grid the terminal manages: hub throughput curves and node transfer rates on the same 61-point timeline.%n%nThe ritual: right-click the hub controller for the node list, then right-click the panel while holding nothing to add grid entries. All screens you placed share one dataset — the whole crossover steam network becomes one wall.%n%nTeleport between nodes reading the trends as you go. That is what a mission control room feels like.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAyg.name=Wireless on Credit
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAyg.desc=Second convergence: the wireless fleet expands on the cat's tab. Pay 1 shimmering coin at the premium counter and take four more Link Anchor (Energy) covers to the machine rows — the economy literally buying infrastructure.%n%nThe ledger stays honest: every new cover refills every 600 ticks at 15 percent downlink loss, buffers V x A x 800 ticks, and never double-counts with cables. Four more machines join the wireless side of the complex.%n%nThe coin handed in is consumed. Credit where credit is due.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAyw.name=Ore Bought with Cats
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAyw.desc=Third convergence: the cluster eats ore, the cat pays for it. Move 64 Neko Coins — one full stack, one shimmering at bank rate — through the vending machine's bulk counter while your Steam Mineral Logistics Cluster runs its chains on the other side of the wall.%n%nThe picture: sign-ins and online hours become coins, coins become ore purchases, ore becomes processed stacks out of the cluster's seven units. The MIAO economy closes its loop through real industry.%n%nThe coins are consumed. Feed the hall.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAzA.name=Turbine to the Grid
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAzA.desc=Fourth convergence: steam becomes EU, EU becomes wireless. Put Link Anchor (Power) covers on your steam-to-EU hardware and let the uplink side of the grid carry the load — uplink loss defaults to zero, and each cover blocks cable output on its side so the watt never gets counted twice.%n%nEfficiency still rules the source: the mega turbine's ceilings run 150 percent on plain steam, 200 superheated, 250 supercritical, 300 dense supercritical — with stack groups adding +10 percent ceiling each and singularity mode a flat +100. The better the steam, the cheaper the wireless.%n%nFour covers on the generator rows and the crossover complex powers itself thin-air style.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAzQ.name=§a§lThe MIAO Industrial Complex
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAzQ.desc=The convergence. One Steam Mineral Logistics Cluster running its seven-unit chains on hub steam; a quantum-anchored ME network storing everything in infinite cells; wireless covers moving every watt; info walls and the machine ledger watching it all; and a cat-signed receipt for 2 shimmering coins on the counter.%n%nEvery strand of this zone physically meets here: GTSR's steam and singularities, GTSWN's wireless telemetry, GTIT's wallet. That was the whole point of the crossover — not three mods in one pack, but one industry with three heartbeats.%n%nBeyond this gate the challenge zone will ask for 32 banked criticals and 300 percent engineering, and the exchange zone will take your surplus. The complex is ready. Are you?
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAzg.name=§c§lGate of Trials
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAzg.desc=The challenge zone: three branches of high-threshold, high-reward trials for the industrial baron. Industrial feats first — endless AE power, integrated processing, 300 percent turbines, banked criticals, full-load drilling. Then the bee branch. Then the gear finales.%n%nRules of the house: rewards here bill in singularities by the eight-stack, circuits by the pair and shimmering coins by the duo. The quests themselves are the trophy — half the prizes are gear you cannot buy.%n%nYou brought the crossover complex with you. Now stress-test it.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAzw.name=Power Reserve
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAAzw.desc=The dream is a creative energy cell; the trial is the honest version — a reserve you actually built. Stack 16 AE2 Energy Cells and 8 Dense Energy Cells into a charged wall: each dense cell is the same footprint with eight times the punch, the classic backbone of any serious ME power room.%n%nTop it with energy cards in your interfaces — they trim the idle draw that eats reserves overnight. The official AE book stacks dense cells for exactly this reason.%n%nShow the reserve to the quest book. Sixteen plain, eight dense, zero excuses.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA0A.name=§4§lOne Roof, Every Machine
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA0A.desc=The integrated processing trial — the old one-click ore processing dream, rebuilt as a megastructure. One Steam Mineral Logistics Cluster, all seven processing units configured — crusher, washer, centrifuge, thermal centrifuge, sifter, magnetic separator, furnace — and all five amplifier units installed and running.%n%nWire the halls to your hub network with the full compartment set: singularity steam and fluid compartments on the input and output sides, mode-locked, talking only to their hubs. Ore enters one door, finished stacks leave the other, whole chains atomic.%n%nHand in the controller, five amplifiers and the compartment quartet. The chain bill is 8,000 x 1.5^(n-1) L/s per chain — budget your steam accordingly.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA0Q.name=Past Three Hundred
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA0Q.desc=Dense supercritical steam alone caps a mega turbine at 300 percent — to go past it you need stack bonuses: 4 stack groups add +40 percent ceiling, tungsten steel pipes +25, every casing tier above 1 adds +5, and singularity mode a flat +100. Build it, feed it, and hold the bar above 300 while it works.%n%nThen install the crown: the Steam Turbine Cycle Overlimit Chip — 64 any-UV circuits, 32 compact fusion coils, 64 critical singularities, 32 field generators VIII, 64 ZPM electric pumps and 1,000mB of organics, a 600-second IV cook. Same steam, more EU; superheated and supercritical condense to distilled water with it active.%n%nScreenshot the efficiency bar. Frame it.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA0g.name=§c§lThirty-Two Criticals
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA0g.desc=The war chest: 32 Critical Steam Entangled Singularities banked at once. Every CSC cycle mints exactly one; the friendly 0.005 x x/(x+5,000) curve means a steady dense stream from the DSM gets you there — eventually, and then some.%n%nStorage discipline: criticals detonate 3 seconds after landing as items, so the vault is machines and hub buffers, never chests. Half a stack of them means every overlimit chip, every reinforced hub bill and every lossless DSM refill is already paid for.%n%nShow the reserve. The quest book will not touch them — probably.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA0w.name=The 240K Club
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA0w.desc=Full-load drilling: a Singularity Drilling Hub pushing a tier-4 miner node at 240,000 L/s of superheated steam — 288x288 area, fortune X, 10-tick cycles — alongside a maxed driller at 8x8 chunks, extraction factor 18.%n%nThe bill at full tilt: 8,000 L/s base plus every active node's tier cost. At tier 4 the miner alone drinks 240K; add the driller's 20K and your superheated plant needs to be the real thing. Idle nodes cost nothing — the club is expensive exactly when it is working.%n%nUpgrades happen in the node GUI or the hub terminal using drill controllers plus 16/32/64 (256 for miner tier IV) singularities. Show both worker nodes and report the load.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA1A.name=Amp It to the Cap
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA1A.desc=Five amplifiers, all running, all tuned: parallel x1.3, speed x1.4, primary output x2.0, secondary output x1.6, steam saver x1.1. Each running amplifier bills fluid by its tier — 4/8/12/16 L/s — and the multipliers land on the steam account.%n%nThe caps are the exam: primary-output boosts take only the highest of their kind, secondary boosts add up, and steam savings cap at 48 percent total. A fully amplified cluster is a different machine than the one you built — the question every cluster engineer answers differently is how much parallel you can afford.%n%nRun all five at once for a full processing session and check in.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA1Q.name=Every Corner Covered
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA1Q.desc=Full-domain wireless: one quantized ME controller anchoring a network of at least eight Quantum Nodes spread across every base you own. Each node carries dense-cable capacity — 32 channels per connection — at 10 AE/t idle; distance limits simply do not apply.%n%nChannel discipline at scale: controller capacity is (faces x 6 minus shared faces) x 32; every node connection brings up to 32 more. At 95 percent the terminal GUI warns you in chat; push past 100 percent and the whole controller structure detonates like TNT.%n%nPlant the nodes, balance the channels, keep every base one click from every other. Coverage is the reward; the reward is also coverage.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA1g.name=§5§lRing Lord, Cell Hoarder
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA1g.desc=The collector's trial, in three relics. First, the Ring of Windrider — the convergence craft: Distant Grasp, Gluttony and Skywalk across the top, Ironheart flanked by two Neko Coins, Dragon's Breath, Mountainbreaker and Tempest across the bottom. The output is creative flight.%n%nThen both infinite cells: the ME Infinity Cell and the ME Infinity Fluid Cell, slotted side by side in a drive. One bar that never moves is luck; two is a policy.%n%nHand in all three. The cats will nod.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA1w.name=§d§lAfter the Master
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA1w.desc=This one unlocks only past the end: the MIAO-GTNH Master quest is complete, every zone gate has lit up, and the challenge zone is what remains. The epilogue trial is a headcount — look around and confirm the wreckage of a full campaign.%n%nWhat should be true by now: a crossover complex humming on hub steam, a bee branch in full production, gear finals worn to the ceremony, and an exchange zone happily liquidating your surplus on cooldown.%n%nThe Master was the gate, not the goal. Whatever comes next starts here.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA2A.name=§2§lBeekeepers, Assemble
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA2A.desc=The bee branch moves in. Start from comb production: hand in 64 combs straight from your apiaries, plus four apiaries and four plain alveary blocks — the swarm's housing stock. An alveary is an apiary with a postgraduate degree: bigger frames, faster queens, industrial output.%n%nThe alveary family has specialist blocks for every job — the plain block is only the beginning of the set this branch will ask for.%n%nGT bees wait down the line; first, the housing. The cats approve of apiaries — they insist on the window seat.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA2Q.name=DOB Dynasty
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA2Q.desc=Breed the DOB bee — gregtech.bee.speciesDob — and hand in a pure-bred drone. The official Bee Breeding book tracks this exact species, so the pedigree is on record: this is GT genetics, not flower arranging.%n%nDrones carry the species on chromosome 0; the quest book matches the primary and secondary allele both. Cross your GT lines until the drone comes out true.%n%nReward includes a pure princess to spare you the walk back.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA2g.name=Salis Mundus Supremacy
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA2g.desc=Breed the Salis Mundus bee — gregtech.bee.speciesSalismundus, the salt-of-the-world strain — and hand in a pure drone. Same rules as the dynasty next door: chromosome 0, both alleles, no shortcuts.%n%nA salt-line colony earns its keep in any chemical branch that drinks sodium; keep the princess busy after the trial.%n%nThe official book grades this one with a double lootbag. The quest book here grades it with singularities.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA2w.name=Shadowmetal Swarm
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA2w.desc=Breed the Shadowmetal bee — gregtech.bee.speciesShadowmetal — and hand in a pure drone. The shadow strain is the metal-dust heart of the GT apiary: its products feed the very alloys your multiblocks are cased in.%n%nPure means chromosome 0 carries the species on both alleles; anything less and the quest book sends you back to the breeding frames.%n%nReward includes a pure princess — a shadow dynasty of your own.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA3A.name=Divided Hive
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA3A.desc=Breed the Divided bee — gregtech.bee.speciesDivided — and hand in a pure drone. The divided strain rounds out the GT quartet this branch demands; its chromosome 0 must carry the species on both alleles, like all the others.%n%nFour species, four dynasties, one megastructure at the end of the branch. Keep the breeding frames warm.%n%nReward includes a pure princess, as is tradition.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA3Q.name=Super Bees
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA3Q.desc=The serum era of the apiary. The Serum Array is the Genetics mod's gene-editing bench: load traits, stamp them onto princesses and drones, and skip generations of patient crossbreeding. Hand one in — a working array, whatever traits you value.%n%nAround it, the classic upgrade stock: circuit boards and genetics parts by the stack, the old super-bee care package. The branch's four dynasties plus a serum bench is an apiary that designs its own queens.%n%nEthics are between you and your bees.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA3g.name=§2§lThe Apiary Megastructure
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA3g.desc=The bee branch's megastructure: nine apiaries and eighteen plain alveary blocks as the housing core, wrapped in the specialist alveary set — swarmer blocks, ender blocks, the whole family on the wall. All four GT dynasties producing at once inside it.%n%nThe math of a mega-apisary: every alveary multiplies queen output, every specialist block bends a rule, and four metal strains in parallel turn the honeycomb into a materials line. The crossover complex's alloy bills start shrinking the day this thing lights up.%n%nHand in the housing stock and check the box. The bees take it from here.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA3w.name=§5§lI Shall Bring Death
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA3w.desc=The gear finals open with the nanosaber: 1,024 iron ingots, 256 stacks of the fine dust and 64 of the rolled stock — the old bill, still eye-watering. The blade that comes out cuts through the late game like etiquette through a meeting.%n%nThe saber rewards patience, not luck: every ingredient is a matter of throughput, and your crossover complex is a throughput machine.%n%nHand in the materials; the blade is yours.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA4A.name=Nano Skin
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA4A.desc=The nano armor quartet — helmet, chestplate, leggings, boots — charged to a million EU each and delivered ready to wear. The old bill was ten thousand coal; this book asks four thousand and change, on the theory that you have a coke hall by now.%n%nNano armor runs on its charge: wear it, feed it, and the damage numbers get politely small. It pairs famously with the saber from next door.%n%nThe set arrives pre-charged. The coal still hurts.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA4Q.name=Twenty Hearts
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA4Q.desc=Ten extra lives for ten heart canisters: hand in 10 BetterQuesting extra lives and walk out with a +20 health ceiling — ten full red hearts on the bar.%n%nThe exchange is deliberately strange: lives for hearts, gamble for permanence. The canisters stay through death; the lives never would have.%n%nCanisters are the quiet kind of endgame gear — no flash, just a health bar that stops lying to you.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA4g.name=Speed Demon Boots
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA4g.desc=The Traveller's Nano Boots: hand in a pair of charged nano boots and sixty-four iron boots as the trade-in stock, and the cobbler returns EMT's masterpiece — nano protection with sprint-step and step-height built in.%n%nThe boots arrive charged to a million EU, same as the armor set upstairs. What they add is mobility: a full block of step height and no fall damage penalties worth mentioning.%n%nThe old trade was boots for boots. This book honors the tariff.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA4w.name=Black & Decker, HSS Edition
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA4w.desc=The power tool trial, high-speed-steel edition: hand in the GT electric tool trio — the drill, the saw and the third cousin from the same charger. The old quest asked for experience points; this book asks for hardware, which is at least falsifiable.%n%nPower tools never break like hand tools — they drain. A full charger dock next to the toolbox is the difference between a workshop and a hobby.%n%nThe set is your graduation from pickaxe anthropology.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA5A.name=Damascus Forever
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA5A.desc=The forge trial: pay the ancient tariff of 64 iron and 16 coal, and the smithy returns four Damascus steel tools — pickaxe, wrench, axe and shears, primary material DamascusSteel, 128,000 durability each.%n%nDamascus steel is the old-world compromise that never needed compromising: hand-tool maintenance with machine-tier endurance. The enchantment on the pickaxe is the smith's signature, not a bug.%n%nThe tools arrive finished. The tariff remains non-negotiable.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA5Q.name=Cobalt Edge
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA5Q.desc=The Tinker's endgame tip: a cobalt pickaxe head. Cobalt sits past the mining-level curve — slap this head on a tool and the question stops being can you dig it.%n%nHand in the machinist's token and a stack of iron; the head comes back in pairs, because a spare edge is a lifestyle.%n%nPair it with the HSS set from next door and the forge chapter of this zone is closed.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA5g.name=§c§lPower Flood
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA5g.desc=The closing trial of the zone — the old diesel gamble, rebuilt on verifiable ground: light up serious EU generation an era or two before the tech tree says you may. The old book asked for a diesel multiblock and paid in casings; this one asks for the steam-to-EU spine itself.%n%nHand in the Gear Steam Compressor and a Mega Steam Turbine Array controller: the compressor turns 25,600 L/s of plain steam into 6,400 L/s of superheated on steel, and the turbine's ceilings run 150 to 300 percent by steam grade. Together they are a power plant that fits in a HV-era yard.%n%nWhen the lights stop flickering, the challenge zone is done. The exchange zone takes it from here.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA5w.name=§6§lGate of Exchange
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA5w.desc=The exchange zone: repeatable counters that liquidate surpluses and restock shortages on cooldowns from one hour to one day. Every counter here is consumption-based — you hand in valuables, the book hands back goods. Nothing is given; everything is traded.%n%nThese counters are the bulk desk of the GTIT trade shop: the shop (tab MIAO Exchanges) handles retail rates all day, while these quests quote wholesale — higher thresholds, richer returns, and a cooldown between visits. Bring volume.%n%nThe door itself only asks that the EV milestone is behind you. Past this gate, the counters do the talking.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA6A.name=§6§lCoins to Ingots
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA6A.desc=The first counter: sixty-four cat coins for sixty-four steel plates and thirty-two steel screws — a full stack of structure plus the fasteners to match. The trade shop sells sixteen plates for twenty coins; the wholesale desk pays one plate and half a screw per coin.%n%nOne-hour cooldown. The plates feed hulls, casings and every retrofit this late in the campaign; the screws keep the maintenance bay honest.%n%nHand over the coins. The pallet lands.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA6Q.name=Surplus Liquidation
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA6Q.desc=The reverse counter: two stacks of steel plates become forty cat coins. The spread is the fee — the shop would charge you a hundred and sixty coins for the same plates, so the buyback desk is where surplus goes to retire, not to turn a profit.%n%nRoughly every three hours. A base that over-produces plates should be piping them here on schedule, not letting them pile into museum wings.%n%nThe plates are consumed. The coins are yours.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA6g.name=§d§lShimmer Buys Singularities
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA6g.desc=The premium counter: twenty-four shimmering coins for eight steam-entangled singularities — a three-to-one rate where the trade shop charges six. The catch is the ticket size and the wait: bulk only, once every two-thirds of a day.%n%nEight singularities at once is a reinforcement hub bill, a drilling-node upgrade, or half an overlimit chip. The wholesale desk assumes you are buying in campaign quantities.%n%nThe coins are consumed on delivery. So is the patience, every sixteen hours and forty minutes.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA6w.name=Singularity Buyback
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA6w.desc=The premium counter in reverse: sixteen singularities become eighty shimmering coins — five per star, against the shop's four, in the same two-thirds-of-a-day rhythm. This is where an over-built singularity plant becomes ring money or chip capital.%n%nThe book takes them all in one delivery; no detonation risk on our counters, the vault behind this desk is made of hub buffers and good intentions.%n%nSixteen in, eighty coins out. The exchange zone thanks you for your service.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA7A.name=Wireless Scrap Desk
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA7A.desc=The recycling counter for the third of the three mods: eight wireless energy covers and eight wireless dynamo covers become fifty-six cat coins — three and a half per cover, where the shop pays three. Legacy network hardware retires here with dignity.%n%nEvery eight hours or so. Retrofit generations of GTSWN covers pile up fast once the quantum terminals take over a base; this desk keeps the drawers clean.%n%nThe covers are consumed. The coins spend anywhere the cats do.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA7Q.name=§5§lShimmer Buys Hub Chips
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA7Q.desc=The premium parts counter: twelve shimmering coins for two reinforced hub singularity chips — six coins a chip against the shop's eight. Reinforced chips are the load-bearing currency of every late-era GTSR expansion.%n%nRoughly every eight hours. Two at a time is exactly one upgraded hub's worth; buy in pairs, expand in pairs.%n%nThe coins go in the drawer, the chips go in the network. Everyone profits, especially the cats.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA7g.name=§6§lEra Certificates
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA7g.desc=The one-time redemption desk: sixty-four LV circuits, sixty-four MV, thirty-two HV, sixteen EV and eight IV — one stack-nostalgia of every era that carried you here — become eight shimmering coins on the spot.%n%nNo repeat: this is a diploma exchange, not an income stream. The circuit towers you minted on the way up each zone are worth a ceremony exactly once.%n%nShow the full spread. The book takes them all, and the shimmer joins your ring fund.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA7w.name=You Will Want These
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA7w.desc=The relic forge: two nether stars and sixteen HV circuits become six nether star plates — the beacons-and-beyond material that never enters a shop. The old service accepted dreamcrafted circuit boards; the new desk takes honest GT HV boards and pays the same.%n%nOnce a day. Nether stars are trade-gated in the shop's final page and wither-farmed everywhere else; the plates stay exclusive to this counter.%n%nStars and circuits in, plates out. Wither says you are welcome.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA8A.name=Alumina Relief Supply
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA8A.desc=The old emergency line, still running: forty stacks of gem dust become twenty-five hundred and sixty measures of alumina — the clay-to-circuit lifeline that saved more MV bases than any tutorial. Ten dust to one alumina, the same honest ratio the original counter kept.%n%nHourly. Gem dust is what a centrifuge sweats; alumina is what an MV smelter drinks. This desk keeps the electrolyzers from ever standing idle.%n%nThe dust is consumed. The MV era keeps moving.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA8Q.name=Quick Travel Depot
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA8Q.desc=The courier desk: sixty-four each of the old tariff metals — the mixed metal and alloy blocks the original contract asked for — become two teleport staffs, fresh off the EnderIO rack.%n%nOnce a day. The teleport staff is the point-and-shoot cousin of the travel network: no anchors, no ceremony, just a rod and a destination. Keep one in the ender pouch and the daily base tour takes ninety seconds.%n%nBlocks in, staffs out. The courier cats salute.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA8g.name=Endless Pearls
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA8g.desc=The pearl assembly line, formalized: sixty-four measures of pearl dust and sixteen sheets of paper become thirty-two ender pearls and four name tags. The old workshop asked for spawn eggs and dye; the new line standardized on dust and paper — inputs a factory actually stocks.%n%nEvery few hours. Pearls are the currency of eyes, wireless and half the endgame; a scheduled pearl run keeps the whole late tree liquid.%n%nDust and paper in, pearls out. The menagerie never needs to know.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA8w.name=Ender Fluid Conduits
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA8w.desc=The dimensional plumbing counter: thirty-two ender pearls and sixteen MV circuits become sixteen ender fluid link covers — the tectech piece that moves liquids across the map like they were adjacent. The old service bundled three different parts lists; this desk takes the two you always have.%n%nEvery eight hours or so. Sixteen covers is a cross-base fluid backbone: boiler halls to hub buffer, one cover seam at a time.%n%nPearls and circuits in, covers out. Distance stops being a fluid problem.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA9A.name=§9§lThe Anchor Network
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA9A.desc=The endgame infrastructure chapter opens with the travel spine: eight travel anchors mounted across your bases, witnessed by two thousand stone bricks of walkway. The old ender-charged contract paid in a full kit; this one pays better and asks you to build the network yourself.%n%nAnchors chain line-of-sight hops: base to hall to hub, each jump a heartbeat. Add the capacitor bank, the wireless chargers and the solar trim from the payout, and the whole compound becomes one walkable machine.%n%nShow the anchors and the road. The kit is yours.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA9Q.name=The Sun Never Sets
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA9Q.desc=A plot of your own: sixty-four solar-grade machines banked as the power plant, and the desk hands over two personal portals — doors to a private dimension where the sun negotiates with nobody. Solar arrays in an eternal noon pay dividends forever.%n%nThe dimension is yours to shape: flat, bright, and free of neighbors with opinions. Mine the overworld in peace, build the cathedral here.%n%nShow the solar stock. The keys are yours.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA9g.name=§b§lFlight, Upgraded
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA9g.desc=The graduation of gravity: show a float core with sixty-four golden carrots, and the desk returns an electric float core — same bauble slot, same sky, but the hunger meter stops being the fuel gauge. The original core traded appetite for altitude; the upgrade trades charge instead.%n%nOne-time. Carrots are the old ritual; the electric core is the new covenant. Below three shanks of hunger the old core cut your wings — this one only asks for a socket.%n%nShow the pair. The sky stops billing you.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA9w.name=The Deep Vault
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA9w.desc=Storage endgame: two quantum link chambers and four IO ports witnessed in place, and the payout is the spatial spine — sixteen spatial pylons and a pair of 2x2x2 spatial cells. A vault that exists sideways of space, with a doorway in your basement.%n%nThe chambers are the same hardware your AE zone finished with; here they graduate from party trick to infrastructure. Pylons on the walls, cells in the drive, junk in the void.%n%nShow the doors. The vault takes it from there.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA-A.name=Ring Buyback Ceremony
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA-A.desc=The one-time trophy desk for the ring era: ironheart, distant grasp, gluttony, skywalk, dragon's breath, mountainbreaker and tempest — one of each, the full pre-windrider set — become twelve shimmering coins. The windrider itself stays on your finger; these are the scaffolding.%n%nNo repeat: retire the set once, with ceremony. Twelve coins is a hub chip upgrade or a third of a critical singularity — the jewelry becomes capital.%n%nHand in the seven. The case closes.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA-Q.name=§e§lThe Graduation Audit
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA-Q.desc=The pre-master checklist: one hub terminal from the steam empire, one quantum terminal from the wireless web, four shimmering coins from the cat economy and one nether star from the wither's estate — a witness from each of the three mods, plus the tax notice.%n%nThe audit does not consume them; it only confirms the portfolio is real. A campaign that can produce all four at once is a campaign that deserves its final gate.%n%nShow the spread. One quest remains after this.
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA-g.name=§6§lMIAO-GTNH Master
betterquesting.quest.TUlBTwAAAAAAAAAAAAAA-g.desc=Every gate is lit: the starter haven, the AE door, seven voltage milestones from steam to luminescent, the crossover complex, the trials, the counters. Twelve anchors, one campaign — and one industrial baron standing in the wreckage of a job extremely done.%n%nYou ran steam empires through GTSR hubs, spread wireless webs over three bases with GTSWN, and made the cat economy hum with GTIT — three mods, one machine, all yours. The singularities kept the lights on; the coins kept the cats in business.%n%nThe graduation prize sits below. Beyond it, the GTIT trade shop's final page opens the moment this quest completes — critical singularities, reinforced chips, infinite cells and the dragon's own egg, priced in shimmer. The campaign is over; the economy is not. Congratulations, Master.
# BQ quests [pack=miao] END

